\par\par \uc2 Grand Central\par \b \uc2 Europe's Central Bankers Hit Calmer Waters\b0\par\par\uc2 2,155 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 2015\par 12:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top North American Equities Stories\par \uc2 DJTNAE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal's Daily Report on Global Central Banks for Wednesday, April 8, 2015:\par \par \uc2 Sign up for the newsletter: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://on.wsj.com/grandcentralsignup"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://on.wsj.com/grandcentralsignup }}}\par \par \uc2 Highlights\par \par \uc2 -- \uc2 Blackstone\uc2 's Take: Europe's Central Bankers Hit Calmer Waters\par \par \uc2 -- Kocherlakota Says Fed Should Be "Extraordinarily Patient" On Rates\par \par \uc2 -- 5 Things to Watch in the Fed's March Minutes\par \par \uc2 -- \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  Votes Down Call to Slash Easing\par \par \uc2 -- Ukraine in Talks to Boost Emergency Cash Reserves\par \par \uc2 BLACKSTONE\uc2 'S TAKE: EUROPE'S CENTRAL BANKERS HIT CALMER WATERS\par \par \uc2 After a topsy-turvy start to 2015, Europe's central banks appear to have found their footing.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday that it hit its monthly target of 60 billion euros ($65.1 billion) in bond purchases in March, the first month of its new quantitative easing program.\par \par \uc2 This occurred despite the fact that government bond purchases only began on March 9, and helped to alleviate concerns that the ECB won't be able to find sufficient assets to buy to meet its ambitious goal of EUR1.1 trillion in asset purchases by September 2016.\par \par \uc2 And things have stabilized in smaller European countries whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been rocked by the ECB's QE program. \uc2 Denmark's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 --which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  massively in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in January and February to keep the krone from strengthening too much against the euro--didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month, the bank said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where a soaring franc early this year fueled concerns of deflation, consumer prices fell less than expected in March on an annual basis, by 0.9% versus market expectations of a 1.1% drop. That will likely reduce pressure on the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  to take additional unconventional easing steps, such as deeper cuts to its already negative deposit rate.\par \par \uc2 This week's reports support the prevailing view that the focus of financial markets will shift away from Europe in coming months and toward the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , which will decide as soon as June whether to raise interest rates for the first time in nearly a decade.\par \par \uc2 Still, if the past few years in Europe are any indication, the calm could prove fleeting. A rising share of the eurozone government debt market trades at negative yields-- Spain joined the club Tuesday with a negative rate on six-month T-bills--which could make it harder down for the road for the ECB to buy large amounts of government bonds unless it pays a high premium for them.\par \par \uc2 And any hiccups from Greece or elsewhere in the eurozone could put renewed upward pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Denmark, Sweden and Switzerland, forcing those central banks to respond.\par \par \uc2 But for now, central bankers in Europe should welcome the reprieve, however long it lasts.\par \par \uc2 -By Brian Blackstone\par \par \uc2 MORNING MINUTES: KEY DEVELOPMENTS AROUND THE WORLD\par \par \uc2 Kocherlakota Says Fed Should Be "Extraordinarily Patient" On Rates. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  should hold off on raising interest rates until the second half of 2016, Minneapolis Fed President Narayana Kocherlakota said Tuesday. "Under my current outlook, I continue to believe that it would be a mistake to raise the target range for the fed funds rate in 2015," he said.\par \par \uc2 5 Things to Watch in the Fed's March Minutes. \uc2 The Fed\uc2  last month gave itself the option of raising interest rates in June, but also signaled it will remain cautious about actually pulling the trigger. More clues about the debate inside the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be revealed when the Fed releases minutes of its March 17-18 policy meeting. Here's what to watch when they come out Wednesday at 2 p.m. EDT.\par \par \uc2 Fed's \uc2 Bear Stearns\uc2 , \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 , \uc2 AIG\uc2  Bailout Records Remain Secret -- Courthouse News Service. \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  memos and spreadsheets regarding emergency loans to three big financial firms during the 2008 crisis are not subject to release under the Freedom of Information Act, a federal judge ruled Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Dollar Coin Loses \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Savings. At one time, the U.S. could have saved billions by ditching the flimsy dollar bill in favor of a durable coin. But for a dollar coin to succeed, the dollar bill needed to be eliminated. Now, the lifespan of paper money has increased, the bulk of the potential savings has been forfeited and the U.S. Federal Reserve, which once supported the change, is against it.\par \par \uc2 ECB Hits Its March Bond-Purchase Target. The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reached its target of EUR60 billion in monthly bond purchases under a new stimulus program launched in March, a sign of resolve that the bank will eventually pump more than EUR1 trillion in freshly created money to stoke the European economy and boost inflation.\par \par \uc2 ECB's Mersch Shoots Down Speculation of Deposit Rate Cut--Boersen-Zeitung. ECB executive board member Yves Mersch rejects speculation the bank could cut its deposit rate below the current rate of -0.2%. He said in an interview with German newspaper Boersen-Zeitung, "From my point of view, a cost-benefit analysis rules out another cut in the deposit rate." --Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  Votes Down Call to Slash Easing. The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's internal split widened Wednesday, as Gov. \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 's toughest critic on the policy board officially called for a steep reduction of its aggressive monetary stimulus.\par \par \uc2 Denmark Stays Out of \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market. \uc2 Denmark's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in March, in a sign that the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stabilized against the euro following weeks of volatility brought on by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stimulus efforts.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Russia Sees No Ruble-Related Risks to Financial Stability. The Bank of Russia sees no risks to financial stability from volatility in the ruble, which has firmed to levels last seen in late December, the head of the monetary policy department at the bank said on Wednesday --Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine in Talks to Boost Emergency Cash Reserves. Ukraine is in talks with several countries to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap lines to boost the country's emergency cash reserves, the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's No. 2 official, \uc2 David Lipton\uc2 , said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Build Portfolio of Chinese Yuan Bonds. The \uc2 Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Tuesday it had decided to build a bond portfolio denominated in Chinese yuan in a bid to diversify its foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets. The move, which will take place in several steps, won't materially influence the adequacy of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves since the portfolio will be relatively small in size, the \uc2 National Bank of Hungary\uc2  said in a statement --Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Hungary \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wants Big Rise in Investment Guarantee Limit. \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants a four-fold increase in the amount of compensation investors would receive for unlawful losses or bankruptcies, the bank said Tuesday, after thousands of retail investors saw big losses following the recent bankruptcies of various locally owned brokerages. The bank has proposed increasing the maximum indemnity payment to 100,000 euros ($109,861) from the current limit of 6 million Hungarian forints ($22,030). --Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 Spain Joins Negative Yield Club. Spain's government on Tuesday issued short-term debt yielding a shade under 0%. The EUR725 million ($796 million) in six-month Spanish debt delivers an average yield to investors of -0.002%. Germany, France, the Netherlands, Belgium, Ireland and Austria already have all their short-term debt trading with yields under 0% in the post-issuance market.\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Still Struggling With New GDP Figures. While it's the job of every \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to find the fine balance between growth and inflation, India's top central banker says he's feeling a bit wobbly, as he's uncertain how to read the country's recently revised gross domestic product numbers.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHIC CONTENT\par \par \uc2 How Rich and Poor Spend (and Earn) Their Money. For many Americans, the rise in food and housing prices is a tough squeeze. That's because--even in an era with low overall inflation--low-income Americans spend a disproportionate share of their money on food and housing. New data from the Labor Department show the extent of the discrepancy.\par \par \uc2 FORWARD GUIDANCE\par \par \uc2 -8 a.m. EDT: Fed's Powell speaks on "Challenges for Monetary Policy" in New York\par \par \uc2 -10 a.m. EDT: \uc2 New York Fed\uc2 's Dudley speaks in New York\par \par \uc2 -2 p.m. EDT: \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  releases minutes from its March 17-18 policy meeting\par \par \uc2 RESEARCH\par \par \uc2 Nominal GDP: Target or Benchmark? Robert L. Hetzel wrote in an Economic Brief from the \uc2 Richmond Fed\uc2 : "Implemented as an activist rule, nominal GDP targeting could be destabilizing. Even if it were feasible, keeping nominal GDP on a preset path in the face of an inflation shock could initiate a recession, while keeping nominal GDP on a target path left unrevised periodically would force changes in inflation if persistent changes in productivity occurred," Still, he wrote, "articulation of a benchmark path for the level of nominal GDP would be a useful start in formulating and communicating policy as a rule. An explicit rule would in turn highlight the importance of shaping the expectations of markets about the way in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will behave in the future."\par \par \uc2 COMMENTARY\par \par \uc2 On Secular Stagnation, \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2 's Theory Meets Larry Summers's Evidence. "Two of the biggest names in macroeconomics met on the battlefield of the blogosphere last week," Greg Ip writes for the Journal. "Former Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2  and former Treasury Secretary Larry Summers were debating why interest rates are so low. .. They both make persuasive (and entertaining) cases. My read is that Mr. Bernanke has theory on his side, while Mr. Summers has the evidence."\par \par \uc2 Should Monetary Policy Take Into Account Risks to Financial Stability? "Let there be no mistake: In light of our recent experience, threats to financial stability must be taken extremely seriously," former Fed Chairman \uc2 Ben Bernanke\uc2  writes on his blog. "However, as a means of addressing those threats, monetary policy is far from ideal. .. In their efforts to promote financial stability, central banks should focus their efforts on improving their supervisory, regulatory, and macroprudential policy tools."\par \par \uc2 America's Endless War Over Money. "The "Audit the Fed" debate is the latest manifestation of a conflict as old as the nation, between those who argue that a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  improves economic stability, and those who see an overbearing government engaged in harmful meddling," K evin Granville and Binyamin Appelbaum write in a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  illustrated timeline of U.S. central-banking controversies.\par \par \uc2 BASIS POINTS\par \par \uc2 - The number of job openings in the U.S. climbed to the highest level in 14 years, surpassing five million for the first time since January 2001. But the number of Americans actually getting hired for jobs fell for the second consecutive month, as did the number of people who voluntarily quit.\par \par \uc2 - Americans pared their credit-card debt in February, the Fed reported Tuesday. Outstanding consumer credit--reflecting Americans' total debt outside of mortgages--grew in February, but the gain was entirely driven by car loans and education debt rather than credit cards.\par \par \uc2 - Aging populations and sluggish advances in worker productivity are binding the global economy to a bleaker, low-growth fate, the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  said in a new report Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 - The \uc2 Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which recently has stepped up its rhetoric warning of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on Wednesday said its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at the end of March edged up slightly on the month in euro terms, but fell when measured in U.S. dollars--Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 - Inflationary expectations in Russia have started to stabilize, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary policy chief said Wednesday, underpinning expectations that more rate cuts are in the pipeline --Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 - The \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  and the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  extended a local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -swap agreement worth A$40 billion (CNY 200 billion) --Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 - Retail sales in the eurozone fell in February after four successive monthly increases, an indication that the boost to the spending power of households from lower oil prices may be waning.\par \par \uc2 - The \uc2 Bank of Portugal\uc2  has a new smartphone app, available in both Portuguese and English.\par \par \uc2 SIGN UP: Grand Central, straight to your inbox.\par \par \uc2 FEEDBACK LOOP: Send us your tips, suggestions and feedback. Write to: Jon.Hilsenrath@wsj.com; Victoria.McGrane@wsj.com; Pedro.daCosta@wsj.com; Michael.Derby@wsj.com; Nell.Henderson@wsj.com; Brian.Blackstone@wsj.com; jason.douglas@wsj.com; Ben.Leubsdorf@wsj.com; Paul.Hannon@wsj.com; Jacob.Schlesinger@wsj.com; Sarah.Portlock@wsj.com; Kate.Davidson@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Follow us on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @WSJCentralBanks, @NHendersonWSJ, @pdacosta, @Blackstonebrian, @PaulHannon29, @michaelsderby, @vgmac, @wsj_douglasj, @BenLeubsdorf, @JMSchles, @MargitFeher, @ToddBuell, @sarahportlock, @KateDavidson\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTNAE1120150409eb490001t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
