\par\par \uc2 Top Stories\par \b \uc2 SNB Ready to Curb Any Franc Strengthening -- Bank President\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neil MacLucas   \par \uc2 416 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2015\par 18:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top North American Financial Services Stories\par \uc2 DJTNAF\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ZURICH--\b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to curb any strengthening of the franc, Swiss National Bank President \uc2 Thomas Jordan\uc2  said Tuesday, adding the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains "significantly overvalued."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Jordan said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will pose "major challenges" for the Swiss economy and the SNB has introduced negative interest rates to blunt its strength. But he offered no new insight as to why the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  scrapped a three-and-a-half-year-old cap on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month, a move that sent it shooting higher and roiled the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The SNB has "lowered rates to unprecedented levels to cushion the effects of the franc's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation and this should have a corrective effect on the franc's valuation," Mr. Jordan said in prepared remarks to be delivered in Brussels on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Jordan's comments come a month after the SNB abandoned a policy of preventing the franc from strengthening above 1.20 francs per euro as investors sought the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a safe haven. The policy was designed to protect Switzerland's export-dependent economy, which is reliant on the eurozone as a market for more than half of its exports.\par \par \uc2 The franc had surged to above parity with the euro when the SNB dropped the cap on Jan. 15. It has since eased to around 1.0600 per euro, but remains high by historic measures.\par \par \uc2 "The Swiss economy is now dealing with very difficult exchange-rate conditions," Mr. Jordan said. "In an interdependent world with highly integrated and liberalized capital markets, the strengths of an economy like Switzerland can, paradoxically, turn into a major challenge."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Jordan said the cap was an "exceptional and temporary measure," repeating comments he has made since the cap was lifted. He added that the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quantitative-easing program would have made the SNB's defense of the cap unsustainable.\par \par \uc2 "The SNB had no choice but to discontinue its policy," Mr. Jordan said. "By maintaining the minimum exchange rate for longer, the SNB would have run the risk of losing control over its balance sheet."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Jordan acknowledged the decision to abandon the cap came as a surprise but said this was unavoidable.\par \par \uc2 "No pre-emptive guidance is possible for this kind of decision, as any guidance would have invited speculative attacks."\par \par \uc2 Write to Neil MacLucas at neil.maclucas@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTNAF1120150217eb2h0001a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
