ry policy. But USD/JPY gains tempered by Japan exporter sales; broadly weaker dollar undertone (ICE spot dollar index last 93.59 versus 94.57 early Thursday) as wider-than-expected U.S. December trade deficit of $46.56 (versus forecast $38.5 billion) and surprise 1.8% drop in U.S. 4Q preliminary nonfarm productivity (versus forecast +0.0%) overshadowed the upbeat jobless claims data and higher-than-expected 2.7% rise in U.S. 4Q unit labor costs (versus forecast +1.2%); positions adjustment ahead of weekend. Other data: 2350 GMT Japan January provisional trade statistics for 1st 20 days, 2350 GMT Japan January international reserves, 0500 GMT Japan December preliminary indexes of business conditions, 2000 GMT U.S. December consumer credit (forecast +$14.5 billion). Daily chart mixed MACD bearish, but stochastics neutral, five- and 15-day moving averages meandering sideways. Resistance at 117.60 (Thursday's high), then at 118.00 (Wednesday's high); breach would expose upside to 118.50 (Jan. 29 high), then 118.66 (Jan. 27 high), 118.82-118.87 (Jan. 23 high-Jan. 20 high), 119.32 (Jan. 12 high) and 119.88-119.97 band (Jan. 9 high-Jan. 8 high). Support at 117.02 (Thursday's low), then at 116.87 (Tuesday's low); breach would target 116.64 (Monday's low), then 115.85 (Jan. 16 low), 115.56 (Dec. 16 reaction low), 115.44 (Nov. 17 low) and 115.13 (100-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD--to consolidate in higher range as markets await U.S. nonfarm payrolls report. EUR/USD supported by waning concerns over Greece as investors reckoned the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to stop accepting junk-rated Greek government debt as collateral for regular \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans could nudge Athens and its creditors toward reaching an early agreement over the country's bailout program. EUR/USD also buoyed by stronger-than-expected 4.2% on-month increase in Germany December manufacturing orders (versus forecast +1.2%); euro demand on buoyant EUR/JPY cross amid positive risk sentiment; broadly weaker dollar undertone. But EUR/USD gains tempered by ECB's large-scale quantitative easing program; positions adjustment ahead of weekend. Data focus: 0700 GMT Germany December industrial production index (forecast +0.3% on-month). Daily chart tilting positive as bullish outside-day-range pattern completed Thursday; MACD and stochastics in bullish mode; five-day moving average rising above 15-day moving average. Resistance at 1.1499 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.1534 (Tuesday's high), then 1.1652 (Jan. 22 high), 1.1680 (Jan. 21 high) and 1.1792 (Jan. 15 high). Support at 1.1390 (hourly chart), then at 1.1304 (Thursday's low); breach would target 1.1278 (Jan. 30 low), then 1.1262 (Jan. 29 low), 1.1224 (Jan. 27 low), 1.1098 (11-year low hit Jan. 26) and psychological 1.1000 line.\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD--to consolidate with bullish bias as markets await U.S. nonfarm payrolls report. AUD/USD supported by Aussie demand on buoyant AUD/JPY cross amid improved risk appetite; broadly weaker dollar undertone. But AUD sentiment dented by weaker-than-expected 0.2% on-month rise in Australia December retail sales (versus forecast +0.4%). AUD/USD gains also tempered by weak iron ore prices (benchmark 62% grade iron fell $0.30 Thursday to near-six-year low $61.10/ton); positions adjustment ahead of weekend. Data focus: 0030 GMT \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  quarterly statement on monetary policy, 0530 GMT Australia January official reserve assets. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics bullish at oversold levels. Resistance at 0.7824 (Thursday's high), then at 0.7848 (Wednesday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.7905 (Jan. 29 high), then 0.8025 (Jan. 28 high), 0.8049 (Jan. 23 high), 0.8135 (Jan. 22 high) and 0.8233-0.8243 band (Jan. 21 high-Jan. 19 high). Support at 0.7732 (Thursday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.7623 (Tuesday's five-and-a-