\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. Consumer Sentiment Boosts USD, Risk Appetite -- ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK\b0\par\par\uc2 1,974 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2015\par 00:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The following are projected trading ranges and outlooks for nine major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs today: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range   Larger Range \par USD/JPY   116.29-117.77    115.85-117.95 \par EUR/USD   1.1459-1.1649    1.1375-1.1792 \par AUD/USD   0.8166-0.8256    0.8130-0.8295 \par NZD/USD   0.7744-0.7856    0.7701-0.7877 \par GBP/USD   1.5073-1.5234    1.5026-1.5273 \par USD/CHF   0.8452-0.8812    0.8350-0.9139 \par USD/CAD   1.1946-1.2045    1.1893-1.2200 \par EUR/JPY   134.70-136.18    134.11-138.88 \par EUR/GBP   0.7593-0.7672    0.7403-0.7746 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent high and lows and technical analysis - Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY--to trade in higher range. Liquidity thin later in global day as financial markets in U.S. are shut for a public holiday. USD/JPY underpinned by yen-funded carry trades amid improved investor risk sentiment (VIX fear gauge eased 6.43% to 20.95; S&P 500 closed up 1.34% at 2,019.42 Friday) on stronger-than-expected rise in \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  preliminary consumer sentiment index to decade-high 98.2 in January from final December reading of 93.6 (versus forecast 94.4), while a 0.8% on-year rise in U.S. December CPI for slowest annual rise since October 2009 stoke expectations that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  could delay raising interest rates. USD/JPY also supported by positive dollar sentiment (ICE spot dollar index rose to 11-year high 93.262 Friday, last 92.65 versus 92.18 early Friday) as above-forecast U.S. consumer sentiment outweighs unexpected 0.1% on-month decline in U.S. December industrial production (versus forecast for no change) and lower-than-expected 79.7% U.S. December capacity utilization (versus forecast 79.9%); higher \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields (10-year at 1.815% versus 1.775% late Thursday); demand from Japan importers; \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's large-scale monetary easing policy. But USD/JPY gains tempered by Japan exporter sales. Data focus: 0200 GMT China December foreign direct investment, 0500 GMT Japan December consumer confidence survey. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, five- and 15-day moving averages declining; but bullish-piercing candlestick pattern completed Friday, stochastics turned bullish at oversold levels. Resistance at 117.77 (Friday's high), then at 117.95 (Wednesday's high); breach would target 118.20 (55-day moving average), then 118.85 (Tuesday's high), 119.32 (Jan. 12 high), 119.88-119.97 band (Jan. 9 high-Jan. 8 high) and 120.68 (Jan. 5 high). Support at 116.29 (hourly chart), then at 115.85 (Friday's low); breach would tilt near-term view negative, targeting 115.56 (Dec. 16 reaction low), then 115.44 (Nov. 17 low), 114.88 (Nov. 12 low) and 113.84 (Nov. 10 low).\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD--to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting 11-year low 1.1459 Friday. Undermined by heightened expectations for full blown quantitative easing measures from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at its meeting Thursday; positive dollar sentiment; fears of Greece exit from the eurozone were the anti-austerity left-wing Syriza party win snap elections on Jan. 25 and renege on the country's reform program; euro sales on soft EUR/GBP cross. But EUR/USD losses tempered by euro demand on buoyant EUR/JPY cross amid positive risk sentiment. Data focus: 0900 GMT eurozone November balance of payments, 1000 GMT eurozone November construction output, 1100 GMT January Bundesbank monthly report. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD bearish, stochastics stays suppressed at oversold levels; five- and 15-day moving averages declining. Support at 1.1459 (Friday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.1375 (Nov. 7, 2003 reaction low), then the psychological 1.1000 line. Resistance at 1.1649 (Friday's high); breach would temper negative near-term view, exposing upside to 1.1792 (Thursday's high), then 1.1846 (Wednesday's high), 1.1859 (Tuesday's high), 1.1871 (Jan. 12 high) and 1.1897 (Jan. 7 high).\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD--to trade with risks skewed higher. Supported by Aussie demand on buoyant AUD/JPY cross amid positive risk appetite; firmer commodity prices (CRB spot index closed up 1.73% Friday at 224.23); Aussie demand on buoyant AUD/NZD cross. But AUD/USD gains tempered by positive dollar sentiment. Data focus: 0030 GMT Australia December new motor vehicle sales. Daily chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish, five-day moving average above 15-day moving average and advancing; although inside-day-range pattern completed Friday. Resistance at 0.8256 (Friday's high), then at 0.8295 (Thursday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.8352 (55-day moving average), then 0.8375 (Dec. 11 reaction high) and 0.8429 (Dec. 4 high). Support at 0.8166 (Friday's low); breach would temper positive near-term view, targeting 0.8130 (Thursday's low), then 0.8066 (Wednesday's low), 0.8031 (five-and-a-half year low hit Jan. 7), psychological 0.8000 line and 0.7700 (July 13, 2009 reaction low).\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD--to range-trade. Supported by Kiwi demand on buoyant NZD/JPY cross amid positive risk sentiment; firmer commodity prices. But NZD/USD upside limited by positive dollar sentiment; Kiwi sales on buoyant AUD/NZD cross. Daily chart mixed as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode, but five- and 15-day moving averages meandering sideways, inside-day-range pattern completed Friday. Resistance at 0.7856 (Friday's high), then at 0.7877 (100-day moving average); breach would target 0.7890 (Thursday's high), then 0.7910 (Dec. 1 high), 0.7926 (Nov. 27 high), 0.7947 (Nov. 21 high) and 0.7974 (Nov. 17 reaction high). Support at 0.7744 (Friday's low); breach would target 0.7701 (Thursday's low), then 0.7688-0.7679 band (Wednesday's low-Jan. 6 low), 0.7616-0.7606 band (Jan. 5 low-Dec. 9 low) and 0.7451 (June 1, 2012, swing low).\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD--to trade with bearish bias. Undermined by positive dollar sentiment. But GBP/USD losses tempered by sterling demand on buoyant GBP/JPY cross amid positive risk appetite; sterling demand on soft EUR/GBP cross. Data focus: 0001 GMT U.K. January Rightmove monthly house price index. Daily chart tilting negative as MACD bearish, stochastics reverting to bearish mode near oversold levels, five-day moving average below falling 15-day moving average and turning downward. Support at 1.5073 (Friday's low); breach would target 1.5032-1.5026 band (Jan. 8 low-July 15, 2013 low), then psychological 1.5000 line and 1.4812 (July 9, 2013 swing low). Resistance at 1.5234 (Friday's high); breach would temper negative near-term view, targeting 1.5266-1.5273 band (Thursday's high-Jan. 6 high), then 1.5336 (Jan. 5 high), 1.5584 (Jan. 2 high), 1.5619 (Dec. 31 high) and 1.5665 (Dec. 22 high).\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF--to range-trade. Liquidity remains thin after \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  on Thursday unexpectedly abandoned the CHF1.20 per euro peg and lowered the interest rate on sight deposit account balances by 0.5 percentage point to \u8722\'22\'120.75%. USD/CHF undermined by franc demand on cross trades versus major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But USD/CHF downside supported by USD bargain hunting; positive dollar sentiment; negative Swiss interest rates; threat of SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Data focus: 0815 GMT \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  December PPI, import price index. Daily chart still negative-biased as MACD & slow stochastic indicators bearish, five- and 15-day moving averages declining. Resistance at 0.8812 (Friday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.9139 (hourly chart, near 61.8% Fibonacci retracement of decline from Wednesday's high of 1.0240 to Thursday's low of 0.7360), then 0.9562 (76.4% Fibonacci retracement), psychological 1.0000 and 1.0220 (Thursday's high). Support at 0.8452 (hourly chart), then at 0.8350 (Friday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.8252 (hourly chart), then 0.8043 (hourly chart); psychological 0.8000 line and 0.7360 (Thursday's three-and-a-half year low).\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD--to consolidate in lower range after hitting five-and-a-half year high 1.2045 Friday. Undermined by loonie demand on buoyant CAD/JPY cross amid improved risk tolerance; firmer oil prices (\uc2 Nymex\uc2  crude settled up $2.44 at $48.69/bbl Friday). But USD/CAD losses tempered by positive dollar sentiment. Data focus: 1330 GMT Canada November international transactions in securities. Daily chart still positive-biased as MACD bullish; stochastics stays elevated at overbought levels; five- and 15-day moving averages advancing. Support at 1.1946 (Friday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.1893 (hourly chart), then 1.1799-1.1792 band (Thursday's low-Jan. 8 low), 1.1728 (Jan. 6 low), 1.1596 (Jan. 2 low) and 1.1563-1.1557 band (Dec. 31 low-Dec. 17 low). Resistance at 1.2045 (Friday's high); breach would reinstate positive near-term view, exposing upside to 1.2200 (76.4% Fibonacci retracement of decline from March 9, 2009 high of 1.3063 to July 26, 2011 low of 0.9403), then psychological 1.2500 line.\par \par \uc2 EUR/JPY--to consolidate in higher range after hitting three-month low 134.70 Friday. Supported by increased investor risk appetite; demand from Japan importers. But EUR/JPY gains tempered by weak euro sentiment; Japan exporter sales. Daily chart still negative-biased as MACD bearish, stochastics stays suppressed at oversold levels; five- and 15-day moving averages declining. Resistance at 136.18 (Friday's high); breach would expose upside to 138.78-138.88 band (Thursday's high-Wednesday's high), then 140.51 (Tuesday's high), 140.88 (Jan. 12 high), 141.36 (Jan. 9 high) and 141.71 (Jan. 8 high). Support at 134.70 (Friday's low); breach would reinstate negative near-term view, exposing downside to 134.11 (Oct. 16 swing low), then 131.18 (Nov. 7, 2013 low).\par \par \uc2 EUR/GBP--to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting near-seven-year low 0.7593 Friday. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics bearish, five-day moving average below 15-day moving average and declining. Support at 0.7593 (Friday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.7403 (Feb. 14, 2008 low), then 0.7340 (Jan. 2, 2008 low). Resistance at 0.7672 (Friday's high); breach would temper negative near-term view, targeting 0.7746 (Thursday's high), then 0.7781 (Wednesday's high), 0.7830 (Tuesday's high) and 0.7855 (Jan. 8 high).\par \par \uc2 Write to \uc2 Jerry Tan\uc2  at jerry.tan@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 18 Jan 2015 18:35 ET  U.S. Consumer Sentiment Boosts USD, Risk Appetite -2-\par \par \uc2 This is a financial news and information service. It is provided in general terms and does not take account of or address any individual user's position. To the extent that this article includes suggestions as to various possible investment strategies which users might consider, it does so in only general terms without reference to the personal factors which should determine any user's investment decisions. Nothing contained in this service constitutes personalized investment advice. \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2  does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors shall not be made the basis for any claim against \uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 . The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article. This article does not constitute or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par