\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-Euro zone's troubles, gold vote test Swiss  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 752 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 2014\par 08:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats, without changes, story first published on Sunday)\par \par \uc2 * Euro crisis prompted 1.20 euro cap on Swiss franc\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk rising again\par \par \uc2 * Gold referendum feeds upward pressure on franc\par \par \uc2 By Alice Baghdjian and Silke Koltrowitz\par \par \uc2 ZURICH, Nov 16 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  faces the biggest test of its cap on the franc in two years but may find it easier to defend now than when euro zone breakup fears were rampant and the strategy unproven.\par \par \uc2 The franc rose to its strongest level since September 2012 on Friday, brushing up against the 1.20 euro limit the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  introduced in 2011 when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength was squeezing exporters and threatening deflation.\par \par \uc2 Renewed weakness in the euro zone, and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's readiness to use radical measures to kick-start the economy, have fired up demand for the franc.\par \par \uc2 A referendum this month on Swiss gold reserves is also playing a role. While approval seems unlikely, a Yes vote would force the SNB to buy gold alongside any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still, capital flows into \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  have declined sharply from the height of the euro crisis, as have fears that the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  would spark inflation.\par \par \uc2 More importantly, the SNB's success in defending the cap back in 2011 and 2012 means it may not have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the same scale to fend off any new market challenges.\par \par \uc2 "The difference between now and 2011 is that the wider markets and most economists believe these \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  can break the neck of speculation," said Rudolf Strahm, an economist and former Swiss lawmaker.\par \par \uc2 "Some market players tried once to attack the franc but all they got was a bloodied nose and lost vast sums of money. Since then no one has dared to try again."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  POINT\par \par \uc2 The franc was trading at 1.2015 per euro by 1200 GMT on Friday, close to the 1.2010 francs per euro level where economists say the SNB has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past.\par \par \uc2 The SNB shocked markets when it imposed the limit in September 2011 after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  shot to record highs against the euro. The following year, as fears of a Greek exit from the euro zone grew, traders unsuccessfully challenged the cap. It has since become the backbone of SNB policy.\par \par \uc2 The latest bout of franc strength is unlikely to prompt such intense purchases, economists say.\par \par \uc2 The SNB poured billions of francs into the market until September 2012 to establish the limit, swelling its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, now around 460 billion Swiss francs ($475 billion) or about 70 percent of gross domestic product (GDP).\par \par \uc2 The cap was breached only once in April 2012, adding credibility to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's pledge to defend the policy with the utmost determination and purchase foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in unlimited amounts to do so.\par \par \uc2 "It is possible that the SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coming days or weeks, but we would not expect the same level of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as in 2012," said Maxime Botteron, a \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  economist.\par \par \uc2 Back then, Swiss investors were repatriating foreign funds out of fear for the euro zone. Anxiety has since subsided.\par \par \uc2 Stagnating prices and faltering economic growth in Switzerland have silenced critics who warned of inflationary risks from the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and has underscored the need for the cap to ward off deflation.\par \par \uc2 The policy has garnered support from exporters and tacit approval from other central banks. The risks from the vast purchases are limited because losses on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are pure accounting losses, economists say, though the bank will need to unwind the reserves at some point.\par \par \uc2 "We really don't see any inflationary pressure yet, even for the next one or two years, so there is no real limit to balance sheet expansion," said Botteron. "From our point of view, the exchange rate floor brings more benefits than costs."\par \par \uc2 This could change quickly if the nation votes on Nov. 30 to oblige the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold at least 20 percent of its reserves in gold, up from 7 percent currently. However, polls show limited support.\par \par \uc2 SNB Chairman \uc2 Thomas Jordan\uc2  has gone out of his way to warn of the risks attached to the proposal.\par \par \uc2 The alternative to \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be negative interest rates. But that could add fuel to Switzerland's real estate boom and prove expensive for its large financial sector. (1 US dollar = 0.9656 Swiss franc) (Editing by Noah Barkin/Ruth Pitchford)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-11-20T07:14:06.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020141117eabh006bu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex10