\par\par \b \uc2 Rates to Zero. What Does Sweden's Riksbank Do Now?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tommy Stubbington   \par \uc2 574 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2014\par 17:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Will \uc2 Sweden's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  be forced to put a lid on its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 That may seem like an odd question to ask when the krona has just plunged to a four-year low against the euro and the dollar. But a growing chorus of analysts is tipping the Riksbank to follow central banks in \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Czech Republic by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to stop its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  growing too strong.\par \par \uc2 The reason is a familiar one for central bankers around the world: chronically-low inflation. Swedish inflation has been stuck close to zero for most of this year, way below the Riksbank's target of 2%. That's why it cut interest rates to zero on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The krona duly tumbled. That should help: a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dampens inflation by making exports more expensive and squashing import costs. But with no room left to cut interest rates further, the Riksbank may have to cook up some new measures if the krona begins to rise again or inflation doesn't pick up.\par \par \uc2 Crucially, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is hostage to developments outside of Sweden. Inflation is tumbling pretty much everywhere--but particularly in Sweden's closest neighbor, the eurozone. And the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already slashed its own rates to negative territory and is widening the scope of its asset purchase program. That puts downward pressure on the euro, effectively lifting the krona.\par \par \uc2 "The Riksbank isn't the only \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  with this problem-- a large number of global central banks are undershooting their inflation targets. [But] Sweden is a small, open economy so it's very dependent on global trade and vulnerable to the spillover effects of low inflation," said Thomas Kressin, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  portfolio manager at \uc2 Pacific Investment Management Co\uc2 ., which has $1.87 trillion under management.\par \par \uc2 If the ECB ramps up its stimulus efforts, Mr Kressin thinks the Riksbank will have to respond, probably by selling krona on the open market. To do so, it could purchase foreign bonds, but would probably stop short of publishing an exchange rate target that could be challenged by speculators, he said.\par \par \uc2 Many think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will go further.\par \par \uc2 "The cut to zero may not have the desired effect; and we expect inflation to remain low over the next few years. If the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  does appreciate against the euro, we may see a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  floor," said James Pomeroy, an economist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It wouldn't be the first. Since 2011, the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  has pledged to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent the euro from trading under 1.20 against the Swiss franc. In doing so, the SNB has accumulated huge stacks of foreign assets. The Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also has a commitment to keep the euro above CZK27 against the Czech koruna.\par \par \uc2 Some think Sweden will avoid monetary policy experiments.\par \par \uc2 "We do not see a repo rate below zero or the launch of unconventional measures. The Swedish economy and the credit-channels are working well," wrote analysts at \uc2 Nordea\uc2  after the rate decision. But if the Riksbank is forced to do more, a lower limit for the exchange rate is the most likely course of action, they added.\par \par \uc2 Expect months of guesswork over what exchange rate might be picked as the limit, if any. \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 28, 2014 12:57 ET (16:57 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141028eaas002vt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
