\par\par \b \uc2 SNB's Jordan says has not  \highlight22\uc2 intervened \highlight\uc2  to defend Swiss franc cap- SRF radio\b0\par\par\uc2 143 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2014\par 11:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2014 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 ZURICH, Sept 18 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to defend its cap on the franc at 1.20 per euro, its chairman \uc2 Thomas Jordan\uc2  told Swiss radio SRF on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "No, we haven't had to enforce it," Jordan replied after being asked whether the SNB had been active in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 "But we did say quite clearly that we are prepared to enforce the cap and if necessary, we will take further measures to do so," Jordan said.\par \par \uc2 The SNB stiffened its resolve to defend the cap earlier on Thursday, toughening its rhetoric as the risk of deflation in Switzerland resurfaces, but it stopped short of announcing any further measures. (Reporting By Katharina Bart) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2014-9-18T10:44:39.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020140918ea9i008dg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
