\par\par \b \uc2 Morning MoneyBeat Europe: Can Yellen Banish Ukrainian Chills? -- WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Cottle   \par \uc2 987 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Februar 2014\par 08:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Good Morning Europe\par \par \uc2 There's the merest hint of cold war chill about markets Thursday morning, with risk aversion ticking up thanks to Russian military exercises near its border with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks managed to hover pretty close to their all-time highs Wednesday, although this risk aversion also saw Treasurys bid, and European stocks are called modestly lower at the open after two days of falls.\par \par \uc2 The morning will see German consumer price and employment data filter out. These are expected to show nothing beyond the outperformance of Europe's engine room and largest economy, and there's nothing new there.\par \par \uc2 Barring any escalation in Ukraine, the day's meat will probably come later when Janet Yellen speaks to the Senate Banking Committee. Markets expect her to imply that it will take much worse news from the U.S. economy than we've seen so far to put the brakes on stimulus tapering.\par \par \uc2 By David Cottle\par \par \uc2 Market Snapshot: U.S. stocks (Wednesday close) DJIA up 0.1%, Nasdaq up 0.1%, S&P 500 flat. Nikkei (Thursday close) down 0.3%. March FTSE now up 0.1%, March S&P up 0.2%. Brent crude down 26 cents at $109.26. Gold up 20 cents at $1,328.20. EUR/USD now at $1.3690, just above $1.3687 late Wednesday in New York. USD/JPY at Yen102.40, just above Yen102.34. Ten-year T-note yields 2.67%, Bund 1.61%.\par \par \uc2 Watch For: German CPI data, labor market stats, French consumer confidence, U.S. durable goods, weekly jobless claims. Fed Chair Janet Yellen reports to the Senate Banking Committee on monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 What you may have missed from MoneyBeat:\par \par \uc2 RBS's Investment Banking Future Lies in its NatWest Past: How quickly the financial industry forgets its own history. Say "NatWest" today and most people will think: retail bank. That's understandable; it is the only form in which the brand survives. But 20-odd years ago NatWest was up to its neck in investment banking. That's worth bearing in mind amid talk of RBS scaling back to its NatWest past.\par \par \uc2 Advantage London in Race to Create New Renminbi Hub: China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is losing ground, but in the race to become an international renminbi trading hub there are few slouches. London has shown signs of stealing a march on its rivals.\par \par \uc2 The Secrecy and Perks Of \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 's Offshore Bank Accounts: Parking millions in a Swiss bank account comes with perks -- and necessitates some secretive behavior, as a report by a Senate investigative panel makes clear.\par \par \uc2 Hector Sants Takes Role at Kate Middleton Art Charity : Hector Sants, \uc2 Barclays\uc2 's former head of compliance and ex-chief executive of the Financial Services Authority, has become trustee a charity to which he donated his entire final bonus when he worked at the U.K. regulator.\par \par \uc2 Bitcoin May Suffer From Mt. Gox Crisis, But Digital \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Will Endure: While the damage to bitcoin from the \uc2 Mt. Gox\uc2  crisis may be severe, the technological advancement represented by digital \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be damaged in the long run.\par \par \uc2 CVC's Koltes - From Plague to Paradise in Europe: Steve Koltes, one of the founding partners of buyout group \uc2 CVC Capital Partners\uc2 , said today that Europe had gone from a place that investors avoided like the plague to a region generating a sense of euphoria.\par \par \uc2 British Shareholders Decline Activist Role: The Shareholder Spring of 2012 hasn't resulted in investors turning into activists, research published in this month's Economic Journal indicates.\par \par \uc2 Commodities Rip Higher. Good News?: Commodity markets have rallied sharply over the past month. That could be good or bad.\par \par \uc2 From The Wall Street Journal:\par \par \uc2 West Deliberates Over Aid As Ukraine's Economy Sinks : Ukraine's prospective Western benefactors suggested Wednesday that it could take months before a major aid package arrives, even as a sinking \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  highlighted fast-eroding confidence in the economy following the ouster of the country's president.\par \par \uc2 Asian Tensions Stoke Japanese Nationalism : A movie glorifying the life of a World War II kamikaze pilot recently topped the box-office charts in Japan for two months. Tokyo book stores have set up corners for titles disparaging Japan's neighbors.\par \par \uc2 Credit Suisse Says Misconduct Limited : The Bank's Chief Executive told lawmakers the bank "regrets very deeply" having aided American tax evasion but said the misconduct was mostly limited to a small group of employees at \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's second-largest bank.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Lowered Yuan : \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  engineered the recent decline in the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of its efforts to prepare the tightly tethered yuan for wider trading, according to people familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking.\par \par \uc2 City of Rome Risks Bankruptcy : The Eternal City, now teetering on the brink of a Detroit-style bankruptcy, has served Italy's new prime minister his first major political headache.\par \par \uc2 Allianz Raises Dividend: Allianz on Thursday raised its 2013 dividend and operating profit target for 2014, after reporting one of the highest operating profits in its history on moderate costs for natural disasters and a solid contribution from financial investments.\par \par \uc2 Telsa Plans $5 Billion U.S. Factory: Electric car maker \uc2 Tesla Motors\uc2  said on Wednesday its proposed battery "Gigafactory" would cost up to $5 billion and allow it to sell as many as 500,000 vehicles a year. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  More at The Wall Street Journal's MoneyBeat blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/moneybeat/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 Order free Annual Report for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse Group AG\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Visit \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://djnweurope.ar.wilink.com/?ticker=CH0012138530"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://djnweurope.ar.wilink.com/?ticker=CH0012138530}}}\uc2 \f2  or call +44 (0)208 391 6028\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Order free Annual Report for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse Group AG\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Visit \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://djnweurope.ar.wilink.com/?ticker=US2254011081"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://djnweurope.ar.wilink.com/?ticker=US2254011081}}}\uc2 \f2  or call +44 (0)208 391 6028\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 27, 2014 02:13 ET (07:13 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140227ea2r000vy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
