\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-SNB denies it is driving down euro zone core yields\b0\par\par\uc2 567 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2012\par 10:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * SNB rejects S&P findings on bond-buying\par \par \uc2 * Bought 80 bln euros of "core" bonds Jan-Jul-S&P\par \par \uc2 * SNB buying exacerbates euro zone bond divergence-S&P\par \par \uc2 (Adding SNB reaction)\par \par \uc2 ZURICH, Sept 25 (Reuters) - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  (SNB) has denied claims by ratings agency \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  that its bond buying was driving down yields for the "core" euro zone states as fundamentally flawed and unfounded.\par \par \uc2 S&P said in a report on Tuesday that the SNB had bought about 80 billion euros ($103.7 billion) of bonds issued by Germany, France, the Netherlands, Finland and Austria over the first seven months of 2012, effectively funding half of their budget shortfall for the full year.\par \par \uc2 However, the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that S&P's report ignored key data and that the assumption that it had bought 80 billion euros of government bonds of the states in question was unfounded.\par \par \uc2 "It (the S&P report) ignores the sizeable increase of SNB deposits with other central banks and international institutions, which are published monthly by the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 ," SNB said in a statement. \par \par \uc2 The S&P report had said that the bond purchases, the result of SNB's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap the Swiss franc by buying euros, are part of the reason for yields for "core" euro zone bonds sinking close to record lows while borrowing costs for other euro zone states remain high.\par \par \uc2 "Largely unnoticed, Switzerland's decision to stem the appreciation of the Swiss franc has led to a de facto recycling of funds from the eurozone periphery to its core, via the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 ," the S&P report said.\par \par \uc2 SNB imposed a cap on the soaring franc a year ago at 1.20 per euro to fight off a flood of money fleeing the euro zone and lessen the risk of the strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  causing deflation and recession in \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend that level pushed SNB's holdings of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 418 billion Swiss francs ($446.4 billion) at the end of August, 71 percent of annual output, though the pace of the rise has slowed as market tensions have eased.\par \par \uc2 The most recent quarterly data to the end of June showed that SNB held 60 percent of its reserves in euros and 22 percent in dollars, with the bulk invested in highly rated government bonds, though SNB gives no detailed breakdown.\par \par \uc2 S&P said that SNB was unlikely to reverse the inflow into core euro zone bonds because central banks are typically hold-to-maturity investors.\par \par \uc2 "However, we also think that this inflow may diminish at some point in the future, potentially leading to upward movement of borrowing costs for some sovereigns that are currently benefiting from peripheral capital flight," it said.\par \par \uc2 SNB Chairman Thomas Jordan said on Tuesday that falling sovereign bond yields supported the view that a gradual recovery in Europe was under way, but added that it was too early to sound the "all clear" and the SNB was still committed to holding down the franc. ($1 = 0.9365 Swiss francs) ($1 = 0.7715 euros)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Emma Thomasson. Additional reporting by Andrew Thompson; Editing by David Goodman)\par \par \uc2 SWISS-SNB/ (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | RNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120925e89p000e3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\p