\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  OMX In SEC Huddle Over \uc2 Facebook\uc2  Plan\b0\par\par\uc2 1,416 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2012\par 16:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 10:39 EDT - Top \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  OMX (NDAQ) executives last week met with SEC Commissioner Troy Paredes to discuss the exchange group's controversial plan to make up a small portion of losses sustained by its members following the problematic stock-market debut of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  (FB) back in May. The meeting, disclosed by SEC, took place Sept 11 and followed new wave of criticism leveled at \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  OMX by firms like \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  (C) and \uc2 UBS\uc2  (\uc2 UBS\uc2 ), both urging regulators to reject portions of the exchange operator's plan to pay firms $62M to help make up an estimated $500M of trading losses stemming from the episode. (jacob.bunge@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:38 EDT - One reason the \uc2 US Department of Energy\uc2  hasn't yet completed its economic assessment of potential US LNG exports may be that it hasn't figured out how to prioritize the 15-bcf/day worth of project applications it's received. Choices include approving only projects with customer contracts in hand, using a lottery or auction to pick winners, and the old-fashioned first-come-first-served standard. When it decides, expect a race to exploit the model or fight it, says David Bloom, partner at \uc2 Mayer Brown\uc2  law firm. "It will engender a race to put applications in front of the DOE" and other regulators, "a race to put up executed contracts with offtakers, and, this being the U.S., a race to the courthouse," Bloom says. (ben.lefebvre@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:37 EDT - A notable big buyer of Treasury bonds in July was Switzerland. Analysts see this as a move by the SNB to park \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  money. Tuesday's TIC data showed that \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  in July added $18.6 billion in Treasurys following June purchase of $10.6 billion. "The Swiss are working to diversify their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves which have become overweighted in euros as part of their 1.200 EUR/CHF" floor, says Adrian Miller, director of global markets strategy at \uc2 GMP Securities\uc2 . He argues that August could see a slowing of TIC buying due to ECB-led improved sentiment. But he expects US assets will continue to draw a premium bid tied to being the asset group that is likely to provide the best opportunity to generate superior risk-adjusted results over the near- to medium-term. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:34 EDT - Apple's (AAPL) shares top $700 for the first time, as its modest gain of 0.5% extends its year-to-date price increase to an impressive 73%. The company's large influence on the broader market is well-known, but for those keeping score, AAPL represents 4.94% of the S&P 500, up from 3.31% at the start of the year, S&P Dow Jones Indices' Howard Silverblatt tells Dow Jones. AAPL is the ninth-best performer in the index this year. Not bad for the world's largest company by market value. (matthew.jarzemsky@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:31 EDT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves have caused a drop in both the cost of default protection on US Treasurys and the volume of protection outstanding. That's despite more borrowing associated with the moves. The cost of CDS is now 31BP, or EUR39,000 a year for five years--the lowest since April and 40% below the cost this time last year. Meanwhile, the value of CDS outstanding has fallen 18% y-o-y. "While the prospect of a weaker dollar is negative for creditors of USD-denominated debt, all else being equal, reduced downside risk in the credit markets from the combined actions of these central banks seem to be outweighing the impact of more dollars," says Otis Casey, Markit analyst. (katy.burne@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:28 EDT - The Dow industrials got a brief pop after home builder sentiment showed a six-year high, but DJIA's hitting resistance at the flat line. DJIA has briefly turned positive a few times since the report, but its trips into the green have been brief. In the S&P 500, the consumer discretionary sector, which includes the home builders, maintains declines after the report, tied with financials as the second-worst performer, behind materials. DJIA now flat at 13552. (matthew.jarzemsky@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:26 EDT - The bond market slightly bounces off session highs in prices as a report adds to optimism on the housing market. The September reading of the NAHB index rose to 40, the highest result since June 2006, showing builder confidence strengthened. Recent reports have shown that the housing market could be a bright spot for the economy, though it remains to be seen how robust the recovery will be and to what extent this sector could boost the broader growth picture. The 10-year is recently 14/32 higher in price to yield 1.787%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:19 EDT - Natural gas -- already edging out coal in US power plants - could start to displace oil in some global industrial sectors. About 19M barrels a day of oil is used for power generation. If natural gas prices remain low and industrial oil users take kindly to the thought of five-year LNG supply contracts, it's a 100M cubic-feet-a-day market waiting to happen. "If LNG supply gets overdone, this market is well suited to step in to absorb the gap," says \uc2 Renato Pereira\uc2 , vice president for origination for U.S. LNG supplier \uc2 Cheniere Energy\uc2 . (ben.lefebvre@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:13 EDT - Jefferies cuts health insurers Molina (MOH) and \uc2 Health Net\uc2  (HNT) to underperform from hold while saying an environment of industry headwinds, plus some company-specific issues, "will expose poor executors." Jefferies believes some companies can deliver solid earnings growth amid turbulence, but prefers big, diversified managed-care firms. MOH, a smaller firm focused on Medicaid, is "fighting battles on several fronts" such as money-losing markets in Texas and Wisconsin and is likely to have negative EPS this year, Jefferies says. HNT, a smaller insurer with commercial and government plans, has run into multiple cost issues this year, and management "hasn't instilled confidence that it can execute," Jefferies says. MOH down 3.1%; HNT down 2.5%. (jon.kamp@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:08 EDT - Pimco's \uc2 Bill Gross\uc2  says it again -- price pressure in the US will rise in coming years and TIPS are a good hedge. In his latest Twitter post, Gross argues "we are in the age of inflation," and that he believes US inflation rate will be 2.5% or higher in coming years. Gross has said consistently in recent months that Fed's monetary stimulus could spark inflation down the road. He told Dow Jones last month he was substantially in TIPS and has recommended investors buy gold and houses as inflation protection. The Fed's QE3 announcement last week has pushed up long-dated inflation expectations -- the 10-year breakeven rate has shot up from below 240bps to around 260 Tuesday just over the past four sessions. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:07 EDT - Swiss pharma company \uc2 Novartis Corp\uc2 . is issuing $2B of debt today, leading a dozen borrowers in a rush to sell bonds. First-time issuer \uc2 FMC Technologies\uc2  (FTI) is selling $800M in a two-part deal, while \uc2 Harley-Davidson\uc2  (HOG) and \uc2 Total SA\uc2  (TOT) are each selling at least $500M of three-year notes. Average yields are just 2.91%, or 0.02 percentage points above the record-low in the Barclays US corporate investment-grade index, and spreads to Treasurys are at a new 13-month low of 1.56 points. That makes borrowing attractive, even though Markit's CDX index, a proxy for risk-sentiment, weakened 2.5% in early trading. (patrick.mcgee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 10:06 EDT - Housing market confidence rises to 40 this month, fifth straight increase. The builders association says tight credit conditions are still hobbling the housing industry. But the Fed's plan to buy mortgage backed securities could lower rates further and put more money into the mortgage system. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 09-18-12 1039ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120918e89i0008c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
