\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: SNB Has Good Reasons to Raise EUR/CHF Floor -Citi\b0\par\par\uc2 1,459 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 2012\par 18:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 12:06 EDT - If the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  raises its floor against the euro for the franc, Citi says doing so would combat worsening deflation, help exporters become more competitive, bolster \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trade balance and reduce SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the move is not risk-free, either. Citi notes a floor-raising could bring Switzerland's reserves close to 100% of GDP, putting the country at risk of accumulating a dangerously large amount of euros and incurring losses if it can't defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg. The investment back sees the peg remaining as is for now, but says the SNB would consider a change given a potential ebbing of the debt crisis. The franc is coming under unusual pressure today against the euro, which is up 0.2% at CHF1.2033, according to EBS via CQG. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:05 EDT - CIBC (CM) "can't see any conclusion in sight" over an Enron-related tax litigation with the \uc2 Canada Revenue Agency\uc2 , CM CEO \uc2 Gerry McCaughey\uc2  says. The CRA issued reassessments disallowing the deduction of about C$3B of the 2005 Enron settlement payments and related legal expenses. Both CM and the Crown are appealing the \uc2 Tax Court of Canada\uc2 's recent ruling striking certain portions of their replies. The appeal is set for Nov. 21. "We're at a very preliminary stage," he says. If CM wins, it'd reap C$395M in a taxable benefit and refund interest. If it loses entirely, it'd incur about a C$989M hit. (caroline.vanhasselt@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:00 EDT - Brazil's raising of import tariffs to "help" its ailing steel sector has been accompanied by a "highly vocal" message from authorities that mills must make a new political commitment not to raise domestic prices, BrasilPlural says. However, while higher import tariffs should initially boost only sales volumes for Brazil's steelmakers, the impact should extend to prices in the medium term, BrasilPlural says. Fears of additional protectionist measures could also contribute to lower import appetite over the next months, ultimately increasing mills' pricing power within the domestic market, BrasilPlural says. (diana.kinch@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:59 EDT - \uc2 Time Warner\uc2  (TWX) is moving back toward last month's 5-year high as \uc2 UBS\uc2  upgrades the media giant to buy and boosts its price target to $50 from $39. Saying a 2-year story is "at early stages of playing out," the investment bank contends TWX's earnings "will be more stable and predictable going forward, benefiting from the upcoming affiliate-fee cycle" that starts for the company in 2014. \uc2 UBS\uc2  also calls TWX "a defensive play given less exposure to advertising vs. peers" while noting the company's share buybacks and dividend could increase further." Finally, "improved programming" should help TWX see advertising growth starting in 4Q "more in line with peers for the first time in several" quarters. Shares are up 3% at $42.58. (kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:59 EDT - \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  sees the following as a likely framework for ECB bond buying: targeting 2- to 3-year maturities, a verbal pledge not to take seniority as a bondholder, refrain from sterilizing purchases for now and condition its purchases on a government also getting EFSF/ESM help. \uc2 Deutsche\uc2  sees specific yield targets or bands unlikely as they're difficult to set and manage over time. Instead, a "soft volume target" is more likely, such as aiming to buy a given percentage of certain maturity buckets. It seems like a EUR180-200 billion buying program is priced into the euro curve, firm says, and sees potential gains in 5-yr Spanish and Italian bonds as private investors step in upon announcement. (cynthia.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:55 EDT - Treasurys are back near session lows after being propped up ahead of the Fed's routine \b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Twist bond purchase. Investors tend to pump up prices going into the buybacks, and today the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $4.74B of notes maturing between November 2020 and August 2022. Dealers put up $11.1B for sale. After the action ended, prices have fallen back down amid market hopes of ECB moves. Benchmark 10-year Treasurys are down 3/32, yielding 1.591%. (cynthia.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:53 EDT - Here were go again. Since the job-search company started considering suitors in March, \uc2 Monster Worldwide\uc2 's (MWW) shares have repeatedly spiked on even the thinnest of takeover rumors. But today there don't even appear to be rumors to reference for MWW's latest pop, up 9.9% at $7.80. The company declined to comment. Analysts are baffled. One of the few things worth noting is MWW's huge short interest; with about 20% of its stock shorted, a short squeeze is certainly a factor. But if you look under the bed or check the closet for a catalyst, it's likely you won't find one. (ben.rubin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:53 EDT - \uc2 Disney\uc2  (DIS) stock earlier hit an all-time high of $51.24 amid momentum that's lifted the shares more than 36% so far in 2012. DIS is the DJIA's biggest dollar gainer today, currently contributing about one-third of the average's 36-point advance. Last month, the entertainment giant reported a 24% increase in F3Q profit, topping most analyst estimates, spearheaded by strong performances at its theme-park unit and a six-fold increase in earnings at its film and TV studios. The studio segment's surge was led by "The Avengers," which underscored the value of DIS's move to acquire \uc2 Marvel Entertainment\uc2  for $4B in 2009. (dwilkerson@marketwatch.com)\par \par \uc2 11:51 EDT - CIBC (CM) CEO \uc2 Gerry McCaughey\uc2  says banks worldwide will face a "continued series of increased requirements" on capital and leverage. "People are underestimating" the global push to hold more capital, he contends during Scotiabank's financials summit. Asked about anticipated surcharges on systemically important financial institutions, McCaughey says, "A small surcharge is something that is excessively optimistic." He professes no direct knowledge of what regulators plan--"I just believe this is a process that's driven by very macro factors and it will continue and go much farther than anyone thinks." (caroline.vanhasselt@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:48 EDT - \uc2 Nokia\uc2 's (NOK) press conference in New York to announce its new Windows 8 phones includes a load of references to the company's homeland. During one presentation, an executive showed photos of an American football game near Espoo, where NOK. He also discussed the need to design phones usable in icy-cold Finnish winters. Other executives used photos of Helsinki to demonstrate whiz-bang technology powering NOK's still and video cameras. However, one thing noticeably lacking is Finns. All five presenters at the gathering--including \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  (MSFT) CEO \uc2 Steve Ballmer\uc2 --are North Americans. (john.stoll@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:46 EDT - The bold call from \uc2 Jim O'Neill\uc2 , chairman of \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  Asset Management, contrasts to those flagging the German court ruling as a big risk. The constitutional court in Germany is scheduled to rule on the legality of the euro zone's permanent bailout fund on Sept 12, and some fear that a "no" vote would derail euro zone's efforts to combat the debt crisis, including ECB's bond-buying plan. Yet O'Neill thinks the court will say yes "because Merkel and her team wouldn't have got themselves into this position if it were at all likely that the Court wouldn't support ESM," he says. It is "red herring, not a big issue," he adds. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 11:41 EDT - The outlook for Argentina's manufacturing sector is improving thanks in no small part to Brazil President Dilma Rousseff. The fortunes of Argentine factories are wedded to those of the Brazil, which bought 40% of Argentina's industrial exports last year. Brazilian industry, and the broader economy, is expected to stage a modest rebound in the months ahead with the help of stimulus measures. Vehicle sales in Brazil hit a record in August thanks to tax breaks. And Brazil's decision to slap extra tariffs on 100 goods imported from outside the Mercosur customs union will benefit Argentina. For many Argentine factories, growth in Brazil trumps effects of double-digit inflation at home. (ken.parks@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 09-05-12 1206ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120905e895000fh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
