\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Swiss exports slip in May on weakening EU demand\b0\par\par\uc2 666 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2012\par 08:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Watches only sector to see growth in exports\par \par \uc2 * Machine exports see sharp declines\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 UBS\uc2  SME barometer shows deterioration in May\par \par \uc2 (Adds details, background, economist quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Catherine Bosley\par \par \uc2 ZURICH, June 21 (Reuters) - Switzerland's exports fell for the third month in a row in May, hit by the strong Swiss franc and weakening demand in the euro zone, raising the likelihood that the country's economic growth will slow in coming months.\par \par \uc2 Exports fell by a real 3.7 percent in May to 17.5 billion francs ($18.5 billion) compared with the same month last year, the Federal Customs Office said on Thursday. Exports to the European Union dropped 10 percent, or by about a billion Swiss francs, while exports to Asia eased 4 percent, the data showed.\par \par \uc2 More than half of Switzerland's exports go to the euro zone, and Swiss companies have been battling a soaring safe-haven franc since the financial crisis erupted in 2008.\par \par \uc2 To prevent Switzerland slipping into recession and deflation, the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  (SNB) in September set a cap of 1.20 francs per euro. But slack demand in euro zone is now giving Swiss firms more of a headache than the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 "Of course the franc does not help, but we've had this level of exchange rate for nearly a year now and the effect at one point is fading," Sarasin economist Alessandro Bee said.\par \par \uc2 "At the moment it's really the global environment rather than the franc."\par \par \uc2 Fears of Greece leaving the euro zone rose sharply in May. This has increased new safe-haven flows into \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  and prompted the SNB to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more heavily to maintain the franc cap than earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Last month also proved gloomy for small and medium sized business - the backbone of the Swiss economy, whose situation declined, a barometer published by \uc2 UBS\uc2  on Thursday showed.\par \par \uc2 TOUGH FOR MACHINES\par \par \uc2 Among the sectors suffering particularly are the machinery sector and the chemicals sector, which includes drugs made by Roche and specialty chemicals by Lonza.\par \par \uc2 According to the trade data for May, exports from the machine and electronics industry sank by a real 15.3 percent from a year earlier, while exports from the metals industry fell 15.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, exporters and the tourism sector called on the SNB to shift its cap on the franc and weaken it further. Those calls have died down of late, however, and the SNB has said it cannot just move the cap at will to any desired level to insulate the economy.\par \par \uc2 "For us, the cap on the franc is justified by the economic situation," said \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  economist Maxime Botteron. "But we don't think the SNB will raise the cap to 1.40. We've seen the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is under considerable pressure of late, I think it would be quite difficult to raise it."\par \par \uc2 Although better-than-expected first-quarter growth prompted the SNB to revise up its growth forecast to 1.5 percent for 2012, Chairman Thomas Jordan said at a policy review last week that growth would slow significantly later this year.\par \par \uc2 Jordan's assessment was mirrored by the ZEW investor sentiment index, which plunged in June and indicates that weak foreign demand will drag on growth in Switzerland in the second half of the year.\par \par \uc2 However exports of watches continued to buck the trend, withstanding the strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mainly because shoppers in Asia value Swiss timepieces for their quality and prestige.\par \par \uc2 Watches, including those made by Swiss-listed firms Richemont and Swatch, were the only sector to see export gains in May. They climbed by 10.2 percent in real terms to 1.911 billion Swiss francs, a faster rate of growth than in April. ($1 = 0.9454 Swiss francs)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Catherine Bosley; Editing by Catherine Evans)\par \par \uc2 SWISS-ECONOMY/TRADE (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | BNX | G | M   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120621e86l00092\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
