\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(4/6) Europe's Markets Are Tested Anew\b0\par\par\uc2 762 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2012\par 01:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par C1\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2012, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL) \par    By Tom Lauricella and Charles Forelle \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New strains surfaced in European markets as a slide in the euro forced \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Switzerland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and Spanish bond yields climbed to their highest level this year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 While trading was thin ahead of the Easter break, traders said the moves reflected further erosion of the positive tone that had supported European bonds, stocks and the euro since the start of the year. On Wednesday, a poor auction of Spanish debt reminded investors that the euro zone's sovereign-debt crisis remains far from resolved despite an enormous injection of cash into the banking system by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The medicine is wearing off," said Richard McGuire, a senior interest-rate strategist at \uc2 Rabobank International\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The euro sank against the dollar, sliding to $1.3066 late in New York, its weakest level since mid-March, from $1.3142 late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 At the end of 2011 and again in February, the ECB provided cheap loans to European banks through long-term refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , known as an LTRO, totaling some $1.2 trillion. For market sentiment, said Mr. McGuire, "LTROs were a sizable boost but only a temporary one."\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, much of the attention was on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, where the Swiss franc lurched higher against the euro and briefly broke through 1.20 Swiss francs per euro, hitting 1.1990 before bouncing back. Traders said the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the franc's rise.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has vowed to keep 1.20 as a line in the sand that it will defend, seeking to halt damage to the Swiss economy from a higher \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which hurts exporters by making their products more expensive overseas. Last summer the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  saw heavy demand as a haven amid worries about the euro zone's debt crisis. The aggressive stance by the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  generally has been considered a success.\par \par \uc2 But investors recently have been pushing the Swiss franc higher amid concern that Europe's economy may be worsening and governments will have a tougher time meeting fiscal targets. Some of the selling of euros Thursday also may have been traders lightening up positions ahead of a four-day Easter break in Europe.\par \par \uc2 Elsa Lignos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  in London, said the pressure is on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  has to step up . . . and make sure it doesn't let its credibility slip," Ms. Lignos said.\par \par \uc2 Stresses also mounted in the European government-bond market Thursday, with Spanish borrowing costs hitting levels last seen before the ECB refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Ten-year yields on Spanish bonds climbed 0.15 percentage point to 5.81%, their highest level since Dec. 1, according to Tradeweb.\par \par \uc2 The LTRO money had been vital to supporting asset prices across Europe in the first quarter, and Italian and Spanish yields fell sharply. But bondholding data show that much, if not nearly all, of the ECB-fueled buying came from domestic banks.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday's auction, at which Spain sold 2.6 billion euros ($3.42 billion) in bonds, near the bottom of what it hoped to sell, suggests that even that demand may be waning. In addition, the ECB's cash injection came in the form of three-year loans, and Spain had to pay much steeper yields to attract buyers for debt that is longer than three years.\par \par \uc2 That could present a problem for Spain. Foreigners have been selling Spanish debt all year, and if domestic banks aren't able to fill the gap, the country could struggle to issue what it needs to in order to cover a budget deficit.\par \par \uc2 "We're seeing some doubts creeping back in about whether or not Spain is going to be able to stick to all its promises," said David Mann, head of research in the Americas for Standard Chartered Bank. There also is worry about "how much Germany is going to pressure them and how much stress that's going to create in the markets," he said.\par \par \uc2 As with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, some traders in the bond market felt moves were being magnified by thin trading in the run-up to the Easter break. Spain also has some room for maneuver. The country already has raised nearly half of its 2012 borrowing needs.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Neil MacLucas, Tommy Stubbington and Neelabh Chaturvedi contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 05, 2012 19:24 ET (23:24 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020120405e845007cp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
