\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Turnover Likely Fell Sharply Into 2012 - BIS Paper\b0\par\par\uc2 566 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2012\par 12:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- Average daily \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading volume hit $4.7 trillion in October before dropping\par \par \uc2 -- Trading volumes declined as the euro zone's sovereign debt crisis intensified\par \par \uc2 -- Higher emerging market bond issuance in the fourth quarter helped offset tight credit conditions\par \par \uc2 (Adds February data from EBS in 6th paragraph, quote from hedge fund manager in 7th-8th paragraphs.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Martin Vaughan and Enda Curran \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading volumes fell sharply toward the end of 2011 as the euro zone's sovereign debt crisis flared up and European banks cut back lending worldwide, the Bank for International Settlements said in its quarterly report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Non-European banks and emerging market bond issues helped to fill funding gaps as European lenders retrenched from global markets in the final part of last year, the BIS said in its report, released Sunday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Daily turnover in global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets likely reached $4.7 trillion on average in October 2011, but volumes likely fell sharply toward the end of the year and into the start of 2012, BIS researcher Morton Bech wrote.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volumes were hit hard through 2011 by the euro zone's debt crisis, as rolling volatility kept investors on the sidelines. Figures from a range of trading centers show volumes during October fell for the first time since 2009, when markets were recovering from the collapse of U.S. investment bank \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lehman Brothers\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The last time the BIS measured daily \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading volumes was in April 2010, in its triennial report. At that time it estimated daily turnover at $4 trillion. The Basel, Switzerland-based BIS describes itself as a banker to central banks, serving as a counterparty for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  transactions and conducting research.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A number of brokers and trading platforms have reported declining \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volumes over the past several months. ICAP, which operates the EBS platform, said last week average daily spot \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volumes for February were $126.7 billion, up 9% from January but still 9% below where volumes were in February 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Beyond the dampening effects of the European crisis, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has also played a role in why volumes have decreased since October last year. Bernard Lock, Asia Pacific head at hedge fund \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Concepts, said that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Japan and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Switzerland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in markets to weaken their respective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  initially caused volumes to spike as traders scrambled to unwind positions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "At first, people tried to go against it, then they rushed to cover their positions and then ultimately they had to surrender," he said. "That $4.7 trillion is quite a special number. We cannot count on seeing that again soon," said Lock.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BIS researchers also found that a roughly 50% increase in emerging market bond issuance in the fourth quarter of 2011, compared with the previous quarter, helped compensate for shrinking available bank credit as a result of European deleveraging.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That helped support asset prices and kept trade finance flowing even as European banks sold assets and reduced lending, the BIS said. "An open question is whether other financial institutions will be able to substitute for European banks as the latter continue to deleverage," the BIS wrote in its quarterly review.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Martin Vaughan and Enda Curran, Dow Jones Newswires; +65 6415 4033; martin.vaughan@dowjones.com [ 12-03-12 1150GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020120312e83c000r8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
