\par\par \b \uc2 Swiss Franc's Bulls Get Ready For SNB's  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 872 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2012\par 19:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By William L. Watts \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FRANKFURT (Dow Jones)--\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Switzerland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is renowned for watches, knives and chocolates. How about bazookas?\f28 \par \par \uc2 A showdown between Switzerland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and investors bullish toward the Swiss franc appears inevitable as the euro sinks ever closer toward a 1.20 franc trading floor officials set last September. That means the deflation-fearing \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  will likely find itself with no choice but to deploy maximum firepower by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily to sell its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , known commonly as the Swissie, versus the euro.\par \par \uc2 The euro on Tuesday traded at CHF1.2038, down 0.1% from Monday.\par \par \uc2 Swissie bulls, who had pressed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to record highs against the euro and the U.S. dollar last summer, stampeded for the exits when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  established the floor, marking one of the most dramatic trading days of 2011.\par \par \uc2 That was a testament to the credibility of the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 , which vowed to halt a rising franc it feared would otherwise sink the alpine country's export-oriented economy into a deep recession and deflationary spiral.\par \par \uc2 Traders remembered the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's earlier \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the franc. While they ultimately failed to reverse the tide, the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  did serious damage to traders caught on the wrong side.\par \par \uc2 The well-timed \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared aimed at creating pain for speculators and touching off chaotic market conditions. And in 2011, the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  appeared poised to be even more aggressive.\par \par \uc2 But things have changed.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, Philipp Hildebrand resigned as chairman of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the wake of a controversy over his wife's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading activity. Vice Chairman Thomas Jordan took the helm on an interim basis as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides on a new permanent chief.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  vowed that its commitment to the 1.20 franc trading floor remained unchanged in the wake of Hildebrand's resignation, stating that it would pursue its policy "with the utmost determination."\par \par \uc2 It was also widely assumed that \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  veteran Jordan would soon be formally named to succeed Hildebrand.\par \par \uc2 That initially kept the Swissie in check. But since then, the Swiss government has indicated that it may take weeks or months to name a permanent successor to Hildebrand.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, critics of the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  have been emboldened, casting doubt on the ability of policy makers to preserve the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's independence, said Peter Rosenstreich, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at SwissQuote Bank in Geneva.\par \par \uc2 To Rosenstreich's thinking, that means the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  will likely have to back up its tough talk with action very soon: A test of the 1.20 franc level appears inevitable, he said.\par \par \uc2 In other words, questions about the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's credibility are effectively contributing to a stronger Swiss franc.\par \par \uc2 That's not the only thing that's topsy-turvy. The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to inject massive amounts of long-term liquidity into the euro-zone financial sector has been cited as a key factor in driving down peripheral euro-zone government bond yields.\par \par \uc2 The injections, much like the U.S. Federal Reserve's full-fledged rounds of quantitative easing, are credited with lifting investors' overall risk appetite. That should be bad for safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Swiss franc and the Japanese yen.\par \par \uc2 But the Swissie and the yen have been rising even as so-called risk assets rally, indicating that some of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's long-term refinancing flows are also leaking into safe-haven assets, said Alan Ruskin, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 "It is unlikely to be a coincidence that the SNB is finding it tough to limit Swiss appreciation after the ECB's generous liquidity additions," Ruskin said in emailed comments.\par \par \uc2 He notes that the normally positive correlations between the euro/Swiss franc pair and bellwether risk indicators like the S&P 500 index have turned negative in the last 30 days, while the correlation between euro/Swiss and Italian bond yields has turned positive, indicating a break in the link between risk appetite and the franc.\par \par \uc2 "This suggests that there is a bigger flow story here regardless of risk, that is going to force the SNB to again massively inflate its balance sheet, this time by being forced to follow the ECB's lead," Ruskin said.\par \par \uc2 While \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders debate where exactly the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  will choose to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Rosenstreich said the prospect for massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has made the euro a good buy as it's fallen below CHF1.21.\par \par \uc2 With the euro hovering around CHF1.205, "we think it's a great buy with 50 pips on the downside and unlimited upside," he said.\par \par \uc2 But traders should be nimble and take profits when the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's inevitable \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  arrives, he said. Over the long run, he questions whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can hold the line at 1.20 if the European debt crisis takes a decided turn for the worse.\par \par \uc2 In other words, subsequent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely to get less bang for the franc.\par \par \uc2 -William L. Watts; 415-439-6400; AskNewswires@dowjones.com [ 01-31-12 1329ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020120131e81v000ir\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
