\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  Keeps EUR/CHF Floor At 1.20\b0\par\par\uc2 728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2011\par 11:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 --SNB keeps EUR/CHF floor at 1.20\par \par \uc2 --SNB says Swiss franc still high at current level\par \par \uc2 --SNB ready to take action if growth outlook worsens, deflation risk rises\par \par \uc2 --SNB expects CHF to weaken over time\par \par \uc2 --SNB points to uncertainty, euro zone risks\par \par \uc2 --Uncertainty for exporters reduced since floor introduction in September\par \par \uc2 --SNB sees growth slowing to 0.5% in 2012\par \par \uc2 (Adds detail, comment.)\par \par \uc2 By Anita Greil\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  Thursday kept the minimum floor unchanged at 1.20 Swiss francs against the euro, even as it warned of a highly uncertain international economic outlook, saying that a further escalation of the European sovereign debt crisis can't be ruled out.\par \par \uc2 Even at the current floor, the Swiss franc is still high and should continue to weaken over time, the SNB said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reiterated that it will defend this level with "utmost determination," and was prepared to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in unlimited quantities if need be.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets have been awash with speculation that the SNB may lift the floor to CHF1.25 or even CHF1.30, because the franc is still trading below purchasing power parity against the euro, and its high value against the euro renders exports expensive.\par \par \uc2 In recent days, however, most economists moved to predict an unchanged floor, saying the high-level of uncertainty in the euro-zone pressures the euro, which would make it tough for the SNB to defend a higher floor. What's more, Swiss government officials repeatedly urged exporters and parliamentarians to stop pressuring the SNB, saying it was important that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can act independently.\par \par \uc2 If the economic outlook and deflationary risks so require, the SNB said it would take further measures.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  obviously highlighted the escalation in the European credit crisis," said Peter Rosenstreich, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 Swissquote\uc2 , a medium-sized Swiss bank. "For us this was the key reason the SNB didn't adjust the EUR/CHF floor higher," he added.\par \par \uc2 At 0935 GMT, the euro traded at CHF1.2272, while the dollar was quoted at CHF0.9446.\par \par \uc2 The euro almost hit parity against the Swiss franc this summer, while the dollar was also trading at a record low. After the SNB set a floor against the euro on Sept. 6, the franc weakened against both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Even if the franc is still high at the current level, planning uncertainty for exporters has been removed because they can now rely on the euro no longer falling below CHF1.20. The SNB has been able to defend the level with surprising ease. Data on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves suggest, that it didn't have to buy much foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the floor, as market participants stayed clear of testing it, knowing that the SNB would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  noted that economic growth in \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  slowed considerably in the third quarter, but kept its growth forecast for this year at 1.5% to 2%. Next year, economic growth will slow to 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 "The international outlook continues to be highly uncertain," \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Philipp Hildebrand said. "A further escalation of the European sovereign debt crisis can't be ruled out."\par \par \uc2 This would have grave consequences for the international financial system. Moreover, given Switzerland's close relations with the euro zone, Switzerland's economic prospects are highly dependent on how the crisis develops, he added.\par \par \uc2 In the near future, there is no risk of inflation in Switzerland, the SNB said. There are, however, downside risks for price stability should foreign demand fall off more sharply than expected, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  added.\par \par \uc2 "In the short term, inflation will dip into negative territory sooner, owing to the effects of the earlier \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, which have been stronger than expected," Hildebrand said.\par \par \uc2 The SNB expects an inflation rate of 0.2% this year, a negative inflation rate of -0.3% next year, and inflation of 0.4% in 2013.\par \par \uc2 The forecast is based on three-months London Interbank Offered Rate at zero and a depreciating Swiss franc, Hildebrand said.\par \par \uc2 The SNB left the target for the three-months London Interbank Offered Rate at close to zero.\par \par \uc2 -By Anita Greil, Dow Jones Newswires, +41 43 443 8044;\par \par \uc2 anita.greil@dowjones.com [ 15-12-11 1048GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111215e7cf000sm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
