\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT: Swiss Franc Buckles After Swiss Price Growth Reverses\b0\par\par\uc2 527 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2011\par 11:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ("UPDATE: Swiss Franc Buckles After Swiss Price Growth Reverses," at 1016 GMT, misstated the date of the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's meeting in the fourth paragraph, due to an error from the source. The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 -- Swiss franc falls sharply after Swiss price growth reverses\par \par \uc2 -- Traders fear further franc-weakening steps from the SNB\par \par \uc2 -- But analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stay away for now\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Eva Szalay \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The Swiss franc fell sharply across the board Monday as traders fled the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after inflation data from the country revealed that the strong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is hitting retailers and pushing the economy towards deflationary territory, but analysts said the weak data print won't trigger more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  yet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Price growth in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Switzerland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reversed in October and showed a 0.1% contraction, as the effects of the safe-haven franc took its toll on the export-led economy and increased fears that the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would take further steps to weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro spiked to CHF1.2394 after the data release, from CHF1.2294, while the dollar traded as high as CHF0.9033 from CHF0.8957, as traders braced for the chance that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  could respond by lifting the euro's minimum level against the franc from the current CHF1.20 level.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "[Inflation] is one of the metrics they're looking at and I think people will be looking to see what [SNB chairman Philipp] Hildebrand has to say after the Dec. 15 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meeting," said Chris walker, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Walker added that as the market is so easily spooked by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fears, the SNB will likely let the market sell the franc of its own accord, before the SNB raises the floor in the euro's value against the franc from the current CHF1.20 level.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We have to bear in mind that it takes at least three months for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  effects to filter through to the economy," said Sara Yates, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London. "I don't think today's print will change monetary policy."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yates added that while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects the SNB to raise the floor in the longer term, it doesn't think the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would plunge in just yet but instead it will likely keep the market nervous. The SNB implemented a price floor under the euro's value at CHF1.20 against the franc in September, after the franc appreciated around 20% against the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from start of the year to September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders have since been fearful of yet more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  steps and regularly sold the franc against the euro when market chatter intensified. Comments like those from SNB Chairman Philipp Hildebrand Sunday noting that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is still too strong have also kept market participants on their toes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 0948 GMT the euro was trading at CHF1.2365, while the dollar was at CHF0.9010.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Eva Szalay, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9305; eva.szalay@dowjones.com [ 07-11-11 1041GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111107e7b7000rl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
