\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  FOCUS: Japan Suffers For Having A Weak  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2011\par 12:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  power remains in political, not BOJ's, hands\par \par \uc2 --Investors not impressed with Japan like they are with SNB\par \par \uc2 --BOJ JGB purchases may help cap yen a little for now\par \par \uc2 By Nicholas Hastings\par \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN\par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are one place where reputation matters.\par \par \uc2 And Japan is finding this out the hard way.\par \par \uc2 The country is currently under the kosh with growth forecasts being cut and exporter share prices tumbling sharply.\par \par \uc2 Why?\par \par \uc2 Because the yen is at the strongest level it has been against the dollar since the Second World War and there is nothing the Japanese can do about it.\par \par \uc2 What is particularly galling for the Japanese is that the Swiss have had the same problem with their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the franc, but they have been able to resolve the issue for now.\par \par \uc2 How come? Because \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets respect the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The difference between the SNB and the Bank of Japan is clear. The former has a long reputation for managing the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when needed.\par \par \uc2 When the SNB said a few weeks ago that it would cap the franc's rise come what may, the markets listened and investors stopped buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite its attractions.\par \par \uc2 The new Minister of Finance Jun Azumi has tried a similar threat, but investors have just kept on buying the yen.\par \par \uc2 This is because, unlike \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the power for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manage the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Japan lies in the hands of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Bank of Japan may control every other aspect of monetary policy but the control of the yen is political.\par \par \uc2 And investors aren't impressed.\par \par \uc2 Azumi may rant and rave about the yen's strength but having just been appointed to his position this summer, the markets really aren't listening.\par \par \uc2 And even if Azumi does unleash \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the yen from rising, investors will more than likely just take the opportunity to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cheaply, assuming that it will continue to rise again anyway.\par \par \uc2 Just look at Capital Economics' updated forecast. It is looking for a dollar down at Y60 in 2013 now. Before, it was looking for a dollar up at Y95 from just under Y76 now.\par \par \uc2 Part of the problem for Japan isn't only the safe-haven role the yen has been given during these times of global uncertainty but also the recent weakness of the dollar.\par \par \uc2 A sharp decline in U.S. Treasury yields, driven by increased speculation of further monetary easing in the U.S., has helped to widen the yield spread between 10-year bonds even more in favor of the yen.\par \par \uc2 A move by the Bank of Japan to narrow this spread, through the purchase of long-term Japanese government bonds, would help to reduce upward pressure on the yen in the near-term at least given the close historical correlation between 10-year yield spreads and the dollar/yen exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 However, any longer-term resolution to the yen's strength through market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as achieved by the SNB in capping the franc, is highly unlikely while the power to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains in the hands of the Bank of Japan's political masters rather than in the hands of the bank itself.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg TNI FRX POV\par \par \uc2 Reuters USD/DJ\par \par \uc2 Thomson P/1066 or P/1074\par \par \uc2 (Nicholas Hastings is a Senior Correspondent in London for Dow Jones Newswires who has written about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for more than 20 years. He previously covered a variety of markets, including equities, fixed income, commodities and energy. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493 or by email: nick.hastings@dowjones.com) [ 26-10-11 1029GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1066   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111026e7aq000v9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw302\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4530\pichgoal600
