\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(9/27) Nissan Keeps Up Pressure On Japan\b0\par\par\uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2011\par 01:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B9\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL) \par    By Chester Dawson \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 YOKOHAMA, Japan -- A \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nissan Motor Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . senior executive on Monday urged Japan to take "decisive" action to prevent the strong yen from hollowing out the country's industrial base, saying he hopes Tokyo can learn from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Switzerland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to weaken its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 The remarks are the latest in an increasingly vocal campaign by Nissan and other Japanese auto makers to push Tokyo into doing more to combat the yen's strength.\par \par \uc2 "The situation requires very determined and decisive action by the government," \uc2 John Martin\uc2 , a corporate vice president at Nissan, said in an interview with The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 "We're very confident that [Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda] is going to learn from this issue with the Swiss franc," said Mr. Martin, referring to the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to set a ceiling for the franc against the euro.\par \par \uc2 The yen has risen to "abnormal" levels that are beyond the ability of any single company to cope with, he said.\par \par \uc2 The comments are the latest warning from Japanese auto industry executives over the strength of the yen, and come after Nissan's chief executive, Carlos Ghosn, said last week that Japanese car makers may be forced into a major strategic rethink if the yen stays near its recent record levels.\par \par \uc2 In a press release on Monday, Nissan Executive Vice President \uc2 Colin Dodge\uc2  also piled pressure on the Japanese government, urging Tokyo to take bold monetary policy steps to halt the yen's rise.\par \par \uc2 "We're praying for the Japanese government to do something in terms of quantitative easing," Mr. Dodge said, referring to steps to slow the yen's ascent by adding liquidity to financial markets.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been under constant upward pressure amid increasing global economic uncertainties, trading near its record high of 75.94 yen against the dollar marked in August. Late Monday in Tokyo, the dollar was at 76.39 yen. The yen was trading at 120 to the dollar as recently as five years ago.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Martin said the success of the Swiss move this month to cap the safe-haven franc's exchange rate against the euro has caused a flight of speculative capital into the yen. That has added to the urgency to switch to using more foreign-made parts at Nissan's domestic assembly lines. A stronger yen makes components made in Japan less price-competitive against those sourced overseas, particularly for use in export vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Nissan plans to increase the percentage of imported parts in models assembled in Japan to as much as 40% for some models produced at its factory in Kyushu, southern Japan, by 2013, up from an average of 28% currently, the executive said. Many of those parts will come from China and South Korea, he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Dodge also said in his statement that while Nissan's annualized global production as of the end of August was 150,000 vehicles greater than at the same time last year, output is still 3.7% below the company's own target. He said he expects Nissan to make up the 80,000 vehicle gap between current production and the company's internal goal "unless there is a massive change in consumer sentiment in the next six to eight weeks."\par \par \uc2 Nissan's shares finished down 3.6% at 620 yen ($8.10) on trading Monday in Tokyo due to continued concerns over the yen's rise and the health of the global economy.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 26, 2011 19:14 ET (23:14 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110926e79q0071t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
