\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Swiss Cap Move Riles Officials In Norway, Canada\b0\par\par\uc2 By Neil Shah and Jessica Mead   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 562 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2011\par 01:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Norwegian and Canadian officials on Thursday criticized Switzerland's move this week to cap the rise of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as the impact reverberated in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets world-wide.\par \par \uc2 The Norwegian krone soared against the euro after the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  said Tuesday that it would use "unlimited" spending to prevent the euro from falling below 1.20 francs. The move sent investors flooding into other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  belonging to economies viewed as fiscally sound, with Norway among the top destinations.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 Norwegian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gov. Oystein Olsen warned investors that a strengthening krone would stifle Norway's economy by hurting exports. A swift policy response--likely lower interest rates--is in the offing if the krone keeps rising, Mr. Olsen said.\par \par \uc2 The Federation of Norwegian Industries said Thursday that the krone's strength was putting pressure on Norway's companies. "It's a serious issue when the Norwegian krone is so strong," Knut Sunde, an executive of the federation said. "We hope that the Swiss effect will fade, but we are not sure," he said.\par \par \uc2 Canadian Finance Minister Jim Flaherty on Thursday said he is "concerned" about Switzerland's audacious move into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and worried it could ignite another round of so-called \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars. Brazil's Finance Minister Guido Mantega last year began referring to the prospect of "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars" among countries seeking to protect their economic growth by keeping the value of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  down.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Flaherty said he planned to discuss the issue at this week's meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers in France.\par \par \uc2 For months, funds have flowed into Switzerland and other supposedly safe countries as investors have sought havens from Europe's financial crisis and the U.S.'s economic woes. In early August, the euro fell to near-parity with the Swiss franc, from 1.50 francs at the start of 2010.\par \par \uc2 Such flows are the latest example of floods of capital leaving the world's sluggish economies--especially the U.S. and the euro zone--and entering faster-growing economies such as Canada and Brazil.\par \par \uc2 What Norway, Sweden, Japan and other refuges have in common is big current-account surpluses, which means they rely less on international investors for financing and seem safer to investors.\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials this week quashed speculation that Japan would follow Switzerland in curbing the yen with a ceiling. However, Norway's pushback is making investors wonder again where to run when markets turn turbulent.\par \par \uc2 "Investors are saying, if you're taking away one of our safe havens, where's the next one?" said Thanos Papasavvas, an analyst at \uc2 Investec Asset Management\uc2  in London, which manages around $100 billion in funds. The Norwegian krone "doesn't have the liquidity and the history of the Swiss franc," he added.\par \par \uc2 It is also possible traders' preferences for Switzerland and Japan won't change much, especially if \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  effort eventually fails, as many expect. The euro traded at 1.2149 francs late Thursday in New York--close enough to the ceiling to indicate there is still investor demand, traders said.\par \par \uc2 --Katarina Gustafsson and Paul Vieira contributed to this article. [ 09-08-11 1908ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110908e798000zj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
