\par\par \b \uc2 Canada Fin Min "Concerned" About Swiss  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Action\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Vieira   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 545 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2011\par 16:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 OTTAWA (Dow Jones)-Canadian Finance Minister Jim Flaherty said Thursday he's "concerned" about Switzerland's audacious move into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and worried it could reignite tensions in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 Flaherty said this will be one of a few "frank" discussions he'll have with his Group of Seven peers as they gather in Marseille for a meeting Friday. He said he'll argue to maintain fiscal-austerity plans and further warn Europe of the need to move boldly and quickly to address its debt woes.\par \par \uc2 Flaherty also told reporters Thursday of his unease with \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and its announcement that it will acquire "unlimited quantities" of euros in an effort to place a cap on the Swiss franc's value. He said he's worried this could ignite another round of so-called \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars, as penned by Brazil's finance minister last year. That could have an impact on the Canadian dollar, which remains at an elevated level and is acting as a drag on the country's exports.\par \par \uc2 "We were concerned when Japan moved unilaterally, and we are also concerned about Switzerland acting unilaterally. That's a discussion I am sure we are going to have," Flaherty said before attending a Conservative Party caucus meeting.\par \par \uc2 The Group of Seven finance and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials are expected to discuss how far they are prepared to go to support flagging growth and avoid a double-dip recession. But Flaherty reiterated the need for major industrialized countries to adapt robust, medium-term deficit-fighting plans, as agreed to at last year's Group of 20 leaders' summit in Toronto, and warned of tension at the G-7 as others argue for a different course.\par \par \uc2 "Our view is the G-20 commitments to move toward deficit reduction and balanced budgets are essential and we want to stay the course. There are some [in the G-7] that have a different view. So we will have an engaging discussion in Marseille."\par \par \uc2 Flaherty is the longest-serving G-7 finance minister, and for months has been calling on Europe to take decisive action to deal with sovereign debt woes to avoid a severe spillover into financial markets. He said he will continue making that case, especially in light of growing doubts about the ability of European policymakers to prevent sovereign defaults.\par \par \uc2 "It is fraught with danger to keep kicking the can down the road. Delay is the enemy," he said, reminding Europe of the bold and quick action the U.S. took in the fall of 2008 to address the credit crisis.\par \par \uc2 While at the G-7, Flaherty plans to hold bilateral meetings with his newly-minted French and Japanese counterparts.\par \par \uc2 Canada had been one of the strongest G-7 economies up until recently, when data showed output shrank in the second quarter. In an interim economic assessment released Thursday, the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development said Canada would recover from the second-quarter contraction to lead expansion among G-7 nations in the fourth quarter.\par \par \uc2 -By Paul Vieira, Dow Jones Newswires: 613-237-0669; paul.vieira@dowjones.com [ 09-08-11 1140ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110908e798000ad\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal25