\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  slip on surprise Swiss move\b0\par\par\uc2 755 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2011\par 18:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Swiss widen "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" with plan to defend franc\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action becoming uncoordinated - economists\par \par \uc2 * Brazil's real, Chile's and Colombia's pesos weaken\par \par \uc2 * Mexico's peso little changed against U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds Mexico peso context)\par \par \uc2 By Jeb Blount\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO (Reuters) - Most Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened against the U.S. dollar Tuesday as a widening world "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" prompted investors to sell emerging market assets and buy more liquid, less risky ones.\par \par \uc2 The battle over exchange rates -- which pits central banks against each other in an effort to promote economic growth and trade -- reached a new level on Tuesday with Switzerland's decision to cap the value of its soaring franc at 1.20 to the euro.\par \par \uc2 With the threat of government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -- some expect Japan to follow \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Switzerland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and protect the yen\par -- many investors will opt to hold dollars, gold, or U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury bonds rather than the Brazilian real, Mexican peso or other Latin American assets, said Andre Perfeito, economist with Gradual Investimentos, which manages about $1.1 billion of assets in Sao Paulo.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We are entering what is clearly a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war, a period of absolute lack of coordination between central banks and international political leaders," Perfeito said. "In a moment like this the move will be to liquidity, safety, away from Latin America."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brazil's real weakened 0.37 percent to 1.6557 to the U.S. dollar, its weakest close since March 28, after plunging as much as 1.05 percent to 1.6670, its weakest intraday level since March 18.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Perfeito expects the real to trade weaker to about 1.70 to the dollar in the coming weeks before tracking back to about 1.60 at the end of the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The immediate move will be weaker, but in the end, \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Brazil's fundamentals -- a high interest rate, stronger growth\par than in the developed world, robust exports -- will keep cash\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 flowing to the country," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The action by Switzerland shows that "the world economic train has no driver and there is no coordinated approach," said Alberto Ramos, Latin America economist with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Until some action is taken to halt the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war, get growth going in developed countries and resolve Europe's sovereign debt problems, investors should be prepared for wide swings in the value of Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the dollar, he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chile's peso fell 0.24 percent to 463.80 to the dollar and Colombia's peso weakened 0.46 percent to 1,791.20 to the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Argentina's official peso , which is managed by regular \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market, weakened 0.12 percent to 4.2075 to the dollar. Argentina's unofficial or parallel peso firmed 0.11 percent to 4.4125 to the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mexico's peso traded 0.13 percent firmer at 12.5100 to the dollar in a day of see-saw trading that saw the Mexican \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slide as much as 0.38 percent.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors were divided between those seeking dollars and other less risky assets and those who sought pesos to invest in higher-yielding Mexican assets, said Jorge Perez-Duarte, a managing director at \f28 \uc2 \f2 TD Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Toronto.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mexico's peso slumped by more than 5 percent in August, its worst monthly drop since 2009, on concern the the United States, which buys around 80 percent of Mexican exports would slip into recession for the second time in two years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even as the peso traded weakly against the dollar, Mexican 10-year "bonos", a government bond in pesos, rose 0.54 to 104.92 percent of face value. The yield fell seven basis points to 6.02 percent. A similar 10-year U.S. Treasury\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 yields 1.98 percent a year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The situation against the dollar is also pretty negative," Perez-Duarte said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the same time, higher returns, faster growth and investment-grade credit ratings available in Mexico and other Latin American countries have led some investors to consider emerging markets as safe havens against slow growth and near-zero interest rates in the United States, Europe and Japan, he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Emerging markets are more or less seen as safe assets to some extent, since that is where the growth is coming from," Perez-Duarte said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Jean Luis Arce and Michael O'Boyle in Mexico City; Editing by James Dalgleish)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110906e796000vi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
