\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ/The Source: SNB  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  May Not Be Enough to Boost Exports\b0\par\par\uc2 602 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2011\par 17:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This article has been posted on The Source, the Wall Street Journal Online's site for European real-time analysis {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/source"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/source}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Posted by Goran Mijuk \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's attempt to cap the franc at 1.20 against the euro should ease pressure on exporters. But many Swiss firms still face an uphill battle as Europe's persistent debt troubles inflict continued pain on companies.\par \par \uc2 "All will depend on how the European debt crisis will evolve," said \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2  analyst Kasper Kirkegaard. "If the troubles increase, especially if global growth slows, more problems could be ahead," he said.\par \par \uc2 Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it will enforce its minimum \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate "with the utmost determination," failure to live up to its word could spell trouble for the economy, said Tobias Straumann, economic historian at the University of Zurich.\par \par \uc2 "Potential difficulties include that the SNB isn't credible enough and will run out of ammunition and there is the danger that the euro assets it will buy to support the franc may be worthless," Straumann said. But overall, the move is "important for exporters and importers to have a certain guidance," he said.\par \par \uc2 Export-dependent companies greeted the SNB decision with a great sigh of relief. "This measure gives us some planning security," said Dominik Werner, spokesman for Swiss chemicals maker Lonza.\par \par \uc2 Lonza had suffered greatly from the strong franc during the past few months, urging it to take over U.S. peer \uc2 Arch Chemicals Inc\uc2  to spread its cost base outside of Switzerland, where it incurs some 40% of its costs today.\par \par \uc2 Even companies that are less dependent on the Swiss franc welcomed the SNB action. "Today's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the SNB can only be supportive for ABB's exports from Switzerland and for Swiss industry in general," said ABB Chief Financial Officer Michel Demare, noting that only a minor part of ABB's cost base is in \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Economists believe the SNB's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should help the overall economy going forward and reduce the risk of bankruptcies and massive job cuts in the months ahead.\par \par \uc2 A majority of Swiss economists expected until recently that gross domestic growth in Switzerland will halve to 1% due to the strong franc. Swiss think tank EconomieSuisse had warned of some 25,000 job cuts if no action was taken.\par \par \uc2 "Many companies are currently planning the budget for 2012 and the fact that they are able now to reckon with a stable franc is making their lives easier," said David Marmet of \uc2 Zuercher Kantonalbank\uc2 . "It could help the economy going forward."\par \par \uc2 Exporters are a main growth driver in Switzerland, contributing more than 30% to the country's annual gross domestic product of around CHF550 billion. Failure to support this sector had risked putting Switzerland into recession, Zurich-based consultancy Wellershoff & Partners said recently.\par \par \uc2 But Europe's debt trouble might still cause problems here and even at the current franc level exporters will struggle.\par \par \uc2 "The franc level is still too high," said Lonza's Mr. Werner, echoing similar comments by Swiss business group Swissmem, which called for more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government action.\par \par \uc2 Rudolf Minsch, chief economist at think tank EconomieSuisse, said that a fair value of the franc to the euro was at about 1.3 francs to 1.4 francs. Below that level, many Swiss exporters will continue to struggle although they have learned to survive, he said.\par \par \uc2 -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 . [ 06-09-11 1544GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110906e79600131\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
