\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ UPDATE: Switzerland Caps The Franc Amid Threat Of More Action\b0\par\par\uc2 By Deborah Ball   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 1,082 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 September 2011\par 14:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  set a limit on how far it will let the Swiss franc rise against the euro, the bank's most aggressive attempt yet to rein in the soaring Swiss franc.\par \par \uc2 The SNB said it would buy euros in "unlimited quantities" should the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall below 1.20 francs, setting the stage for what could be a long battle by the bank to defend its action in the face of surging concerns about debt problems in the euro zone and the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The SNB said Tuesday that it would "no longer tolerate" the euro falling below the minimum rate. In a statement, it said will enforce the limit with "the utmost determination and is prepared to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in unlimited quantities."\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 The euro surged 10% to CHF1.22 on the news. Prior to the news, the euro had sunk to about CHF1.10, after hitting a record low of CHF1.001 in early August. The Japanese yen also weakened, as investors wondered whether the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 --which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  early last month to rein in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --might follow the Swiss.\par \par \uc2 Even gold, which has hit repeated highs this summer, lost some of its safe-haven shine, falling 2% early in the European day.\par \par \uc2 In its first comment on the Swiss bank's efforts to rein in the franc, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it took note of the decision to set a floor against the euro. In August, ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet urged countries to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in tandem, rather than going it alone.\par \par \uc2 In August, the SNB slashed interest rates to close to zero and flooded the market with liquidity in an effort to pull down a franc that has threatened to choke off Swiss growth. The franc gained 27% between November 2010 and early August against the euro, with investors viewing Switzerland--with its strong growth and solid public finances--as a haven from the euro zone's festering debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 The SNB's August moves helped drive the euro nearly 20% higher against the franc, but in recent days a deteriorating outlook for the global economy and the euro zone have sent the franc higher again. That may have prodded the SNB to take further action, say \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists.\par \par \uc2 The relentless strength of the franc has already pushed some weaker Swiss exporters into bankruptcy, and sent others scrambling to slash prices to hold onto business. Swiss export prices fell 6.2% in the first half of the year. The Swiss government expects 1.5% gross national product growth in 2012, down from 2.1% this year and 2.7% last year.\par \par \uc2 For the last month, the Swiss government and business leaders have been appealing for bolder action by the SNB to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly amid signs that growth elsewhere in Europe and in the U.S. could hit the skids. In its Tuesday statement, the SNB said the franc "poses an acute threat to the Swiss economy."\par \par \uc2 Having drawn a line in the sand, the SNB could now face an endurance test in defending its euro-franc limit, as investors increasingly worry whether key euro-zone members such as Italy will execute a credible plan to get their enormous debt under control.\par \par \uc2 Recent experience is not encouraging. When the franc began surging in early 2009 in the early days of the euro-zone crisis, the SNB regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros. But it abandoned the effort in June 2010 in the face of widespread criticism. Between March 2009 and June 2010 the euro fell from CHF1.48 to CHF1.32, and the SNB posted 2010 exchange-rate losses of CHF32.7 billion. The losses sparked calls for SNB President \uc2 Philipp Hildebrand\uc2  to resign.\par \par \uc2 This time, the SNB will be fighting a sharp slowdown in the U.S. economy as well as the deep debt crisis among its euro-zone neighbors, in particular Italy. The ECB recently warned Rome that it may not be doing enough to get its debt under control, and a general strike Tuesday in Italy highlighted the difficulty of reforming public finances there.\par \par \uc2 "This could be a bloody battle for the SNB over the next few months," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for \uc2 Rabobank\uc2 . "It's a battle of the SNB against the search by investors for safe havens."\par \par \uc2 Others point to the strong language in the SNB's Tuesday statement as a sign of the bank's determination, which could bolster its credibility in the market. The language is far starker than the bank's communiques to the market during its 2009-2010 \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The SNB is now completely committed," said Alessandro Bee, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Bank Sarasin\uc2 . "There is no going back. They will do everything to defend this. They have to resist the pressure. Otherwise, they can just close the SNB."\par \par \uc2 Bee expects the euro to stay quite close to the CHF1.20 level in the coming months, and then appreciate to around CHF1.30 next year if the euro-zone debt problems ease.\par \par \uc2 Should the SNB have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for years, it could risk driving up inflation. But economists see virtually no short-term risk of rising prices; Swiss inflation has been around 0.5% over the last year. Instead, if the SNB posts further losses due to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it could cause tensions with the local leaders of \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 26 cantons, which have traditionally received a large portion of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's profits. However, early comments from politicians in Bern were supportive of the SNB's move.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Swiss business welcomed the news, although some said a euro-franc exchange rate of 1.20 still leaves them under pressure.\par \par \uc2 "We think a fair value would be 1.30 to 1.40 francs, but taking into account the serious debt crisis in Europe and the problems in the U.S., we believe 1.20 francs is a level that can be easily defended," said Rudolf Minsch, chief economist for Swiss business lobby Economiesuisse in an interview. "There is a trade-off in order to convince the market that this level is defensible."\par \par \uc2 -By Deborah Ball, The Wall Street Journal; Deborah.Ball@dowjones.com [ 09-06-11 0837ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110906e7960004x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
