\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: S&P/ASX 200 Offshore Leads Bullish; SPI Up 0.7%%\b0\par\par\uc2 1,666 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2011\par 01:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2323 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's overnight SPI 200 futures rose 0.7% to 4319.0 amid strong gains on European equity markets and moderate gains on Wall Street due to month-end portfolio rebalancing and stronger-than-expected U.S. economic data. The FTSE 100 rose 2.4%, the DAX rose 2.5% and the STOXX 600 rose 2.9%, boosted by U.S. July factory orders rising 2.4% vs 1.9% expected, and Chicago August manufacturing PMI at 56.5 vs 53.5 expected. August ADP private sector jobs missed expectations, but the main thing tempering early strength was U.S. regulatory blockage of \uc2 AT&T\uc2 's takeover of T-Mobile. Spot gold fell to US$1,825.80 from US$1,835.60, and U.S. 10-year bond yields fell 5 bps to 2.23%. S&P 500 Financials rose 1.2%, Materials rose 0.3%, with \uc2 BHP Billiton\uc2  (BHP.AU) ADRs at equivalent A$39.85 vs A$39.74 locally, and Energy rose 0.7%. Nymex crude oil settled down 9 cents at $88.81. LME copper rose 1.2%. The S&P/ASX 200 closed Wednesday at 4296.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2323 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude is likely to trade with risks skewed to the upside after hitting a four-week high of $89.54/bbl Wednesday, as long as the price can hold above Wednesday's low of $87.67, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. The daily continuation chart is still positive-biased as the MACD and stochastic indicators are bullish, but the latter is at the overbought level. A rise above the $89.54 resistance, which is near the previous base set on June 27, would expose upside to the psychological $90.00 line, and then to $92.59 (the Aug. 4 high). But a drop below the $87.67 support would temper the near-term positive outlook, opening the way down to $86.46 (Tuesday's low), then to $85.11 (Monday's low), $82.95 (Friday's low) and $81.18 (the Aug. 22 low). October crude is down 9 cents at $88.72/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2321 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at nine foreign brokerages placed net sell orders for 6.4 million Japan shares before the market open, according to traders. The 24th consecutive day of pre-market selling may be negative for the market, although the yen value of the figures is unknown. Sell orders totaled 17.6 million shares, while buy orders amounted to 11.2 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2320 GMT [Dow Jones] LME base metals are likely to be mixed with weak economic data from the U.S. offset by supply problems in some of the metals. ISM reports Chicago Business Barometer fell to 21-month low; ADP's employment report overnight shows U.S. private-sector payrolls rose 91,000 in August vs expected 100,000. However, the threat of a possible strike at \uc2 Freeport McMoran Copper & Gold\uc2 's (FCX) Grasberg mine in Indonesia, the second work halt in as many months as contract-renewal talks stall, could keep copper prices well supported. LME metals closed higher Wednesday despite cautious trading with 3-month copper closing up 1.2% at $9,274/ton and aluminum closing up 1.9% at $2,469/ton. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  expects copper to target $9,350/ton next and look for $9,600/ton and expects aluminum to move toward $2,500/ton-$2,540/ton. (arpan.mukherjee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2317 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional markets may rise, tracking Wall Street's gains Wednesday, after a batch of U.S. economic data topped expectations. Metal sector stocks may be in focus as their U.S. counterparts got a boost after \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  analysts said they expect demand for metals to remain "fairly healthy," driven by emerging markets. Indian, Indonesian and Malaysian markets are closed. In the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the Swiss franc may retreat on profit-taking after rising more than 2% against the USD and EUR Wednesday amid declining expectations the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rein in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on disappointment with \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's package of aid to support its economy. The USD/CHF is at 0.8067 vs 0.8059 late Wednesday in New York and the EUR/CHF is at 1.1597 from around 1.1579. The EUR/USD is at 1.4373 vs 1.4370, the USD/JPY is at 76.74 vs 76.66 and the EUR/JPY is at 110.29 vs 110.22. A raft of data is on the slate, including South Korean August trade data at 0000 GMT, China's August CFLP PMI at 0100 GMT and its \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI data at 0230 GMT, Australian retail sales data at 0130 GMT, India August manufacturing PMI at 0500 GMT and Thailand August CPI at 0700 GMT. In Europe, Germany's 2Q GDP data and eurozone August PMI are due. In the U.S., initial weekly jobless claims and the ISM manufacturing report are due. (leslie.shaffer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2313 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs are likely to open lower on weakness in U.S. Treasurys Wednesday and caution ahead of a closely-watched 10-year JGB auction later in the day. But JGBs are likely to rebound in the afternoon, with the 10-year sale expected to meet firm demand as secondary market JGB prices fell Wednesday, says \uc2 Tokai Tokyo Securities\uc2  chief strategist Kazuhiko Sano. The yield rise may spur buying on expectations of a higher coupon in the auction and higher yields in the secondary market. Lead September JGB futures are tipped in a 142.30-142.51 band vs 142.41 at the last close, while the 10-year cash JGB yield is tipped in a 1.020%-1.045% band; it was at 1.030%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2307 GMT [Dow Jones] The AUD/USD looks especially vulnerable if retail sales due at 0130 GMT are weaker than the market expected +0.2%, says \uc2 Westpac\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Sean Callow. "July retail sales will be particularly closely watched, after marked weakness in recent months. Sales have fallen in three of the past four months, a great focus of local media and source of defensiveness from the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 ." The AUD/USD is at 1.0692. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2304 GMT [Dow Jones] South Korea's inflation breached the 5% level for the first time since the second half of 2008, driven by high food prices, highlighting local authorities' struggle to anchor prices. Data released by Statistics Korea Thursday shows August CPI rose by 5.3% on-year, compared with a 4.8% rise tipped by a Dow Jones Newswires poll, marking the eighth consecutive month inflation has breached the Bank of Korea's 2011 inflation target range of 2%-4%. The August print is the highest since a 5.6% on-year rise in August 2008. But it's unclear whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will move to tighten policy given heightened external uncertainties and the government's expectation CPI growth will fall into the 3% range come September. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2258 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei may rise following better-than-expected U.S. economic data released Wednesday, including Chicago-area manufacturing figures and factory orders data. "There is still a lack of buying incentives at home so overseas developments continue to be closely eyed with focus on the U.S. ISM and jobs data (due later Thursday and Friday)," says Hiroichi Nishi, general manager at SMBC Nikko Securities, pegging the index range at 8900-9050 vs Wednesday's close of 8955.20 (+0.01%). He says the upside for the index will continue to be limited following a 3.7% gain over the past 5 sessions, but notes that around 65% of TSE 1st section shares continue to be priced below book value. Nikkei 225 September futures ended Chicago trading at 9030 vs the Osaka close at 8960. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2255 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's 2Q Terms of Trade data adds to an improving economic picture, with the merchandise terms of trade index increasing 2.3% from the first quarter, outstripping the median forecast for a 1.7% rise in a Dow Jones Newswires poll of eight economists. The index was once again boosted by dairy export prices, which rose 4.5% on the quarter. Export volumes were up 0.5% and are currently at their highest level since the series began in the June 1990 quarter. The data will likely add to the view that the economy is pulling out of the doldrums and may lead more economists to reconsider a possible rate hike at the Reserve Bank's September meeting rather than expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to remain on hold until December. (Rebecca.Howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments and share their views on financial news topics. Please email us at: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    TalkBackAsia@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2254 GMT [Dow Jones] Tread cautiously in chasing the CHF surge after the Swiss government cut its support package for its economy, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Sean Callow. "We would be wary of pushing this too far, though, as Swiss economy minister Schneider said the franc remains "massively overvalued"." The EUR/CHF is at 1.1599. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 31, 2011 19:23 ET (23:23 GMT)\f28 