\par\par \b \uc2 SNB, Swiss Government Act To Curb Franc Amid Calls For Stronger Measures\b0\par\par\uc2 973 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2011\par 19:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --SNB boosts liquidity for third time to weaken franc\par \par \uc2 --Swiss government agrees to CHF2 billion aid package\par \par \uc2 --SNB fails to mention peg or limit for franc gains\par \par \uc2 --Market disappointed about measures, questions SNB\par \par \uc2 --SNB ready to take further measures if necessary\par \par \uc2 By Goran Mijuk and Neil MacLucas\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  and the Swiss government Wednesday took separate steps to limit the impact of the strong franc on the country's economy, but their latest measures failed to impress the market and triggered calls for more decisive action to restrain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The SNB took a third attempt in as many weeks to curb the franc by boosting liquidity, lifting sight deposits by two-thirds to 200 billion Swiss francs ($253 billion). But even as it warned of further action, its latest move failed to rein in the franc and disappointed market participants who questioned the institute's credibility as many had expected a peg of the franc to the euro.\par \par \uc2 In a separate move, the government said it will provide the economy with a CHF2 billion aid package partially aimed at helping exporters and the tourism industry. Both sectors risk losing business to foreign competitors and may shed thousands of local jobs, said Economy Minister Johann-Schneider-Ammann. The aid package will be financed out of the country's expected budget surplus this year, he said.\par \par \uc2 Despite the latest measures, the franc remained high versus the euro and the dollar, trading at CHF1.1406 and CHF0.7894, respectively. This unnerved investors who had expected a peg, said Sebastien Galy, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . "Everybody is shaking their heads at this. The SNB implicitly told the market that there would be a peg, and now the market is disappointed," he said.\par \par \uc2 As a result, traders, economists and lobby groups in Switzerland called for more action, including asking for a temporary peg or a floor of the franc to the euro. "Some sort of targeted limit for the franc against the euro would be the most appropriate measure in these difficult times," said Rudolf Minsch, chief economist of influential business lobby group EconomieSuisse. "The Swiss economy simply can't live with a [the current franc rate], and it will have serious consequences for exports and employment."\par \par \uc2 While some left-wing political groups such as the Swiss Federation of Trade Unions have called for a Swiss franc peg to the euro at CHF1.40, economists say that the SNB is much more likely, if at all, to target a lower level that reflects the fair value between the pair. According to Nicolas Wiedenmann, economist at \uc2 Bank Vontobel\uc2 , the franc's purchasing-parity fair value against the euro stands at about CHF1.28. To achieve this target, economists say a series of SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be needed.\par \par \uc2 Such steps, even if contested by some economists as they are considered risky and costly, are still possible even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday failed to use this tool after the bank's director \uc2 Thomas Jordan\uc2  last week said that temporary \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were in line with the bank's mandate, which raised hopes for a peg.\par \par \uc2 But the expectations for more direct action remained alive as the government said it plans to set up a task force, a move that was interpreted as a potential signal that more measures may be envisaged. "We are in a special situation, which requires a special response," said Economy Minister Johann Schneider-Ammann, warning that the current financial turmoil is likely to curtail global growth and add further stress to the Swiss economy.\par \par \uc2 Since the outbreak of the financial crisis, the government has launched several aid packages, which have helped the economy weather the crisis well and reach growth rates of more than 2% and keep a lid on unemployment. Schneider-Ammann, however, made it clear that the government won't interfere with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it comes to monetary decisions.\par \par \uc2 The SNB, which once again said that the franc was "massively overvalued," left the door open for more action as it warned that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "will, if necessary, take further measures against the strength of the Swiss franc." In its previous two moves, the SNB cut interest rates close to zero and added liquidity to money markets, effectively pressuring short-term interest rates into negative territory. The SNB had in the past said that the liquidity boost needed time to take effect.\par \par \uc2 While the market expects more action, timing is considered to be decisive and an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or peg could come in the weeks or months ahead. "The SNB can still take more decisive action and it may be that at the current levels they don't yet want to use more drastic measures in order to remain flexible," said \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  strategist Antje Praefcke.\par \par \uc2 But for such a step to have effect, the situation in Europe needs to improve, economists say. "The current SNB measures should continue to have an effect on the franc, even if the real medicine is probably a return of confidence in the neighboring euro zone," said Julien Manceaux, economist at ING.\par \par \uc2 Still, pegging the Swiss franc to the euro seems unrealistic for \uc2 UBS\uc2 , which said that "imminence of any such project [is] overblown." Although the political establishment in \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  has changed its view about direct \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , prior SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves in 2009 and 2010 had inflated the bank's balance sheet and led to hefty book losses. The consideration of a peg, critics say, could trigger an avalanche of Swiss franc buying and put further pressure on the SNB and trigger renewed political criticism.\par \par \uc2 -By Goran Mijuk, Dow Jones Newswires; +41 43 443 80 47; goran.mijuk@dowjones.com [ 17-08-11 1747GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110817e78h0015c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
