\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Swiss Job Market Depends On CHF, Global Economy - Official\b0\par\par\uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2011\par 10:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 8 Aug 2011 04:11 EDT *DJ  Swiss Govt Offical: Economic Facts Not As Bad As Sentiment\par \par \uc2 8 Aug 2011 04:15 EDT DJ  INTERVIEW: Swiss Job Market Hinges On Franc, Global Economy\par \par \uc2 -- Swiss job market depends on franc, global economy\par \par \uc2 -- Economic facts aren't as bad as sentiment\par \par \uc2 By Goran Mijuk\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--Failure by the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  to act to prevent the franc from rising and a worsening of the global economy are likely to badly hit the Swiss job market, the Swiss government's Director of Labor Serge Gaillard said Monday.\par \par \uc2 "The impact of the strong franc would be grave should the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remain at current levels and if the SNB would fail to achieve a change of the trend," Gaillard told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview.\par \par \uc2 "It could hit many sectors such as the tourism and banking industry," said Gaillard, who heads the Labor Directorate at Switzerland's government State Secretariat for Economic Affairs, or SECO. "But it would be most felt with exporters."\par \par \uc2 Switzerland's jobless rate is currently at a two-and-a-half year low of 2.8%, but still off the level reached before the financial crisis when the figure stood at around 2.5%. Concerns are growing that a rising Swiss franc, which is trading at record levels against the dollar and the euro, could badly hit the country's exporters, a main source of economic growth in \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many economists expect the SNB to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week said it will take action if needed. While some economists believe the SNB could introduce negative interest rates to stem the flow of safe-haven buys, others believe that direct \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are possible even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2009 and 2010 left it with huge losses, which sparked political criticism.\par \par \uc2 But despite the challenging situation, Gaillard said the institute is yet maintaining its 3.1% jobless rate expectation for 2011 as the global economy is still in a healthy shape. "As far as growth is concerned, the situation in economies in Northern Europe and the U.S. is solid. The facts aren't as bad as the sentiment," Gaillard said.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland's unemployment rate remained unchanged in July at 2.8% compared with June. But analysts noted that forward-looking figures were somewhat less strong compared with earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 "While the number of job seekers continued to decline, the number of open positions was down by a pronounced 9.6% on the month, following a decline by 2.9% month-on-month in June," said \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . "Meanwhile, the number of short-time workers continued to head lower and is now only slightly above pre-crisis levels."\par \par \uc2 -By Goran Mijuk, Dow Jones Newswires, +41 43 443 80 47; goran.mijuk@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 08, 2011 04:15 ET (08:15 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110808e788002k2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
