\par\par \b \uc2 US Stocks Stumble At Open, Oil And Euro Fall As Gold Soars\b0\par\par\uc2 By Javier E. David   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 972 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2011\par 15:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Investors roiled by recessionary fears exited from risk-related assets Monday, seeking shelter in safety instruments as fallout from the credit dowgrade of the world's largest economy continued to reverberate in global markets.\par \par \uc2 On the heels of a decision by \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  to strip the U.S. of its triple-A credit rating, markets around the world trembled. Equity markets in Asia, Europe and the Middle East suffered steep losses in offshore trading, while high-yielding assets such as oil and the euro skidded lower, while gold--a haven for nervous investors--surged to new record highs.\par \par \uc2 Europe's own debt travails and a torrent of data suggesting that the world's economy is decelerating sharply have unsettled investors, and left some analysts weighing whether S&P's move could become the tipping point for a global recession.\par \par \uc2 "We're clearly in the midst of an all-out panic," said Bruce McCain, chief investment strategist at Key Private Bank. "There's nothing that says this better than the fact that U.S. credit has been downgraded and we're running up the price of U.S. Treasury bonds."\par \par \uc2 In recent trade, the benchmark 10-year note rose 1 4/32 in price, pushing the yield down by 12 basis points to 2.429%. The yield tumbled to 2.334% Friday, the lowest level since October. Bond prices move inversely to their yields.\par \par \uc2 The 30-year bond was 1 3/32 higher to yield 3.765%. The two-year note was 3/32 higher to yield 0.248%.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average had dropped 319 points, or 2.8%, to a session low. The Dow posted its biggest weekly point loss since the financial crisis in 2008 last week, falling 5.8% as investors fretted about the slowing U.S. economy and Europe's spreading sovereign-debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 The Standard & Poor's 500 stock index was off 3.4%. The technology-oriented Nasdaq Composite was down more than 3%.\par \par \uc2 The S&P downgrade was largely telegraphed ahead of time, allowing investors to consider a world where the U.S. was no longer a triple-A country. As a result, Treasurys and the dollar began Monday on a relatively strong note.\par \par \uc2 Europe, however, is not much better off than the United States, with European officials struggling to contain a raging debt crisis. As a result, the euro was roundly battered by risk aversion, and fears that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to safeguard Italy and Spain from contagion risks through purchases of their debt would fall short of what analysts believe is necessary.\par \par \uc2 The ECB "is going to be facing a huge battle about how much they are going to buy" in Italian and Spanish debt, said Brian Kim, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBS Securities in Stamford. Meanwhile, "the dollar is doing better than people thought it would be."\par \par \uc2 The euro shed more than two cents from its Asia trading high against the dollar to change hands around $1.4175. The dollar set a new record low against the Swiss franc at CHF0.7629 and fell nearly one per cent against the yen to Y77.70, but its losses were limited amid fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the monetary authorities of Japan and \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Commodities linked to global growth tumbled Monday, with declines spread among oil, copper, grains and other agricultural products. Crude oil was recently down $3.82, or 4.4% at $83.06 a barrel on the \uc2 New York Mercantile Exchange\uc2 , flirting with eight-month lows. Copper fell 0.7% to $4.0865 a pound.\par \par \uc2 The declines reflect fears that a possible second recession due to political deadlock in the U.S. and a worsening sovereign debt crisis in Europe will eventually curtail demand for raw materials.\par \par \uc2 "Nobody knows what will happen and as a result they prefer to limit losses," said Remy Penin, commodity market strategist at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The uncertainty has sent investors flooding into gold, with spot prices hitting record highs above $1,700 a troy ounce in Europe Monday. Gold futures for December delivery, the most actively traded contract, were recently up 3% to $1,700.80 a troy ounce, after hitting an all-time high of $1,714.30.\par \par \uc2 High-grade corporate bond prices fell, and an index tracking risk in the market rose to its highest level in 10 months. Risk premiums--the added yield that investors demand to own corporate debt instead of safer Treasurys -- shot up significantly on each of the five most-active bonds in the secondary market. The premium on \uc2 Capital One Financial\uc2  4.75% bonds due 2021 rose by 22 basis points and \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  8.5% bonds due 2019 widened by 32 basis points. The CDX index that measures risk in the market rose by four basis points to 107. A basis point is one-hundredth of a percentage point.\par \par \uc2 But many think the dislocation will be a temporary one and some surmise the action might actually be beneficial for higher rated bonds. "Very highly rated corporates, including the three triple A rated companies (Exxon, J&J, and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 ) as well as strong double A rated firms, may actually see spreads narrow as investors look to high quality alternatives to Treasuries," according to Guy LeBas, chief fixed income strategist at Janney Montgomery. He also notes that lower- rated credits could tread wider due to volatility brought on by greater risk aversion.\par \par \uc2 -By Javier E. David, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-4564; javier.david@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 --Steven Russolillo, Min Zeng, Kellie Geressy, Jerry Dicolo contributed to this article. [ 08-08-11 1035ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110808e7880002y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
