\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ SNB's Jordan: Watching Franc, Euro Developments Closely\b0\par\par\uc2 661 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2011\par 18:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 13 Jul 2011 12:57 EDT DJ  Jordan: Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 CentraL Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Watching Franc Developments Closely\par \par \uc2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  is closely watching \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for developments involving the Swiss franc and euro, the vice chairman said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "We are of course concerned about the debt crisis in the euro region" and its impact on the franc, \uc2 Thomas Jordan\uc2  said during a panel discussion on how the euro-zone debt crisis could affect Switzerland.\par \par \uc2 The franc has gained sharply in recent weeks versus the euro as investors seek a refuge from the ongoing debt crisis in the euro region.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen about 11% versus its European and U.S. counterparts in the past three months, undermining the competitiveness of Swiss exports in its most important markets.\par \par \uc2 Both the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the economics ministry have warned the strong franc is likely to slow the pace of growth in one of Europe's best-performing economies this year.\par \par \uc2 -By Neil MacLucas, Dow Jones Newswires, +41 43 443 8046;\par \par \uc2 neil.maclucas@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 13 Jul 2011 13:07 EDT *DJ  Jordan: SNB Can Take Measures If Deflation Signs Emerge\par \par \uc2 13 Jul 2011 13:08 EDT *DJ  Jordan: SNB Isn't Powerless In Face Of Strong Swiss Franc\par \par \uc2 13 Jul 2011 13:14 EDT *DJ  Jordan: Franc-Euro Peg Idea Would Mean Loss Of SNB Autonomy\par \par \uc2 13 Jul 2011 14:40 EDT DJ  UPDATE:SNB Watching Swiss Franc Closely; Rejects Euro Peg Idea\par \par \uc2 (Recasts throughout, adding detail and comments.)\par \par \uc2 By Neil MacLucas\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  is watching the Swiss franc's strong appreciation closely but Vice Chairman \uc2 Thomas Jordan\uc2  on Wednesday rejected suggestions it should peg the franc to the euro.\par \par \uc2 "We are of course concerned about the debt crisis in the euro region" and its impact on the franc, but calls for the SNB to peg the franc to the euro would mean the loss of its own autonomy, Jordan said during a panel discussion on how the euro-zone debt crisis could affect \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to check the franc's gains between March 2009 and June 2010 but stepped back from this policy, citing the disappearance of the danger of deflation.\par \par \uc2 The franc has gained sharply in recent weeks versus the euro as investors seek a refuge from the ongoing debt crisis in the euro region, and various ideas have been proposed to try to offset the effects of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the Swiss export sector.\par \par \uc2 One proposal is for the SNB to peg the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the euro, but Jordan said this would mean the SNB surrendering its mandate and autonomy to the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We would need a change in the SNB's constitution to allow such a move," Jordan said.\par \par \uc2 Still, the SNB isn't powerless in the face of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market developments, and if signs of deflation emerge in Switzerland, it can take measures, Jordan said. He declined to say whether such measures included \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and stressed the SNB's main goal is the preservation of Swiss price stability.\par \par \uc2 Jordan said it is in everyone's interest that the European Union find a solution to some countries' debt problems and that if the situation worsens, then Switzerland, too, would have large problems.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen about 11% versus its euro-zone and U.S. counterparts in the past three months, undermining the competitiveness of Swiss exports in the country's most important markets.\par \par \uc2 Both the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the economics ministry have warned the strong franc is likely to slow the pace of growth in one of Europe's best-performing economies this year.\par \par \uc2 -By Neil MacLucas, Dow Jones Newswires, +41 43 443 8046;\par \par \uc2 neil.maclucas@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 13, 2011 14:40 ET (18:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110713e77d0052y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2175\pichgoal600
