\par\par \b \uc2 Barron's(7/11) European Trader: Swiss Franc: Overheating\b0\par\par\uc2 1,387 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Juli 2011\par 06:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par M7\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From BARRON'S) \par    By Jonathan Buck \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A sharp rise in the value of the Swiss franc means bad news for the Swiss economy, but it offers intriguing possibilities for investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The franc is soaring largely because it is considered a safe harbor by investors. At a time when the euro zone is grappling with a consuming debt crisis and parts of the Middle East are in turmoil, investors want to park their cash in low-risk locales, and Switzerland is considered one such destination. Since the start of the year, the lure of the franc has pushed up its value by 24% against the euro and 12% against the dollar. Adjusted for inflation, the Swiss franc is trading at its highest level in 30 years.\par \par \uc2 "It has gone beyond what you would normally expect," says Paul Robson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBS in London, who says he fears for the health of the Swiss economy in the next 12 months.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland's cabinet discussed the strong franc last week, and its impact on the economy, noting that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in "an overvalued phase." The government forecasts gross domestic product will grow by 2.1% this year, after trimming its 2012 forecast last month to 1.5% from 1.9%.\par \par \uc2 Typically there is a lag between exchange-rate appreciation and the effects on the economy. Butalready there are signs in Switzerland of a slowdown in the export sector, and corporate profit margins are coming under pressure. Zurich's benchmark SMI equity index has lost more than 3% in value since the start of the year. In contrast, Germany's blue-chip DAX index has risen more than 8%. The impact on equities may be muted due to the global nature of Swiss companies' businesses, says Frances Hudson, global strategist at Standard Life in Edinburgh. Food giant \uc2 Nestle\uc2  (NESN.Switzerland), with 458 factories around the world, said first-quarter sales were reduced by 10% due to the strength of the franc. Its shares are down about 3.6% this year.\par \par \uc2 Previous efforts at \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been ineffective in curbing the franc's rise. "For the short term, the strength of the franc is justified," says Hudson. "Long- term, it is questionable."\par \par \uc2 The franc is likely to remain strong against the euro if the euro-zone problems intensify, but could depreciate swiftly if the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon finds a solution to Greece's woes. That seems unlikely for the moment. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  strategists believe the base case is that the euro zone's issues recede but not quickly, and the value of the euro rises to close to 1.40 Swiss francs in four years from about CHF1.20 Friday.\par \par \uc2 Hudson suggests the Swiss franc could get some relief against the dollar if the U.S. Congress reaches a budget deal before its Aug. 2 vote on raising the government's debt ceiling. She notes investors can earn "positive real yields" on Swiss franc bonds. RBS's Robson suggests that investors short the Swiss franc and buy Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly the Singapore dollar, which he describes as "a good trade over the long term."\par \par \uc2 European markets sank Friday after the release of disappointing U.S. June jobs data. The Stoxx Europe 600 closed at 273.76, down 0.8% on the day and 0.4% on the week. One group that came under increased pressure: Italian banks, on speculation that many would fail the latest round of European stress tests. \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  (UCG.Italy) ended Friday at 1.23 euros, off almost 8%.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Buck is a news editor with Dow Jones Newswires in London.\par \par \uc2 --- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                    Dow Jones Wilshire Global Indexes \par  \par Region/ \par Country \par  \par DJ Global                  DJ Global    DJ Global    DJ Global \par Indexes                    Indexes      Indexes      Indexes       Point Chg.   % Chg. \par Local Curr.                U.S. $                                  From         From \par  07/08/2011   Wkly % Chg   07/08/2011   Wkly % Chg   on 12/31/2010 12/31/2010   12/31/2010 \par  \par Americas \par                               3624.24        0.31     3412.82      211.42        6.19 \par  \par Brazil \par    93869421.59     -2.64     23653.71       -3.16    24874.17    -1220.46       -4.91 \par Canada \par        4425.73      0.61      5302.47        0.42     5193.84      108.63        2.09 \par Chile \par        8760.31     -0.88      7093.78       -0.17     7391.23     -297.45       -4.02 \par Mexico \par       23104.1      -0.86      6096.58       -0.64     6119.2       -22.62       -0.37 \par U.S. \par       14115.22      0.49     14115.22        0.49    13131.47      983.75        7.49 \par Latin America \par                              10200.22       -2.22    10657.36     -457.14       -4.29 \par  \par Europe \par                               2935.51       -1.76     2779.57      155.94        5.61 \par  \par Austria \par        2789.62     -2.17      3098.14       -3.69     3065.28       32.86        1.07 \par Belgium \par        3170.82     -0.65      3523.32       -2.19     3382.71      140.61        4.16 \par Denmark \par        4503.68      0.37      5094.38       -1.18     5006.27       88.11        1.76 \par Finland \par        9241.39     -1.22      9179.37       -2.74    10019.09     -839.72       -8.38 \par F