\par\par \b \uc2 SNB Pushed To Peg Swiss Franc; Seen Asserting Independence\b0\par\par\uc2 830 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2011\par 16:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --SNB pushed to peg Swiss franc by left wing\par \par \uc2 --Industrial lobby backs calls for temporary Swiss franc peg\par \par \uc2 --SNB seen resisting franc peg, other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves\par \par \uc2 --Swiss franc has gained 8.9% against the euro this quarter\par \par \uc2 By Katharina Bart\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  is under pressure from Switzerland's left-wing party to consider pegging the Swiss franc to the euro to dampen the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rapid appreciation.\par \par \uc2 It's an unconventional step which reflects growing political support for the country's increasingly vocal exporters.\par \par \uc2 Left-wing calls for Switzerland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to temporarily fix the Swiss franc to the euro in case the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthens further underscore the increasingly politically sensitive nature of the franc's strength against the euro and the U.S. dollar. The Swiss franc has strengthened 8.9% against the euro thus far this quarter, and 9.3% against the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The call for the franc to be pegged garnered support from Swiss industrial lobby group Swissmem, which echoed its calls for the SNB to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exporters.\par \par \uc2 Still, the SNB is unlikely to be pressed into the unconventional step--even as an emergency, temporary measure--because it would mean compromising its independence on monetary policy decisions, according to experts.\par \par \uc2 "I cannot envisage this being feasible politically, because one of the SNB's greatest virtues is its ability to act independently in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and monetary policy issues and react to Swiss inflationary and economic developments as it sees fit," \uc2 Bank Sarasin\uc2  strategist Ursina Kubli said.\par \par \uc2 The SNB is also likely to be too bruised from its unsuccessful 2009 \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to consider a peg, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts. The Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance sheet is bloated from spending billions of francs attempting to curb the franc's surge against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the U.S. dollar and euro. As a result, the SNB posted a 21 billion Swiss francs ($25.3 billion) loss in 2010.\par \par \uc2 The franc has Switzerland's exporters up in arms because a strong Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  eats into their profit margins. Still, the SNB isn't likely to be swayed by what is turning into an increasingly political issue, the experts said.\par \par \uc2 "I think this is more a political platform to prepare the ground for the upcoming campaign for the fall elections," Wellershoff and Partners senior economist Felix Brill said.\par \par \uc2 Leading left-wing politician Christian Levrat Sunday warned Switzerland risks losing 100,000 jobs because of the Swiss franc's strength against the euro. Switzerland's political establishment is gearing up for October 23 parliamentary elections, and criticizing the SNB after the ill-fated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is proving fertile ground.\par \par \uc2 SNB president \uc2 Philipp Hildebrand\uc2  has already come under attack from former justice minister Christoph Blocher, who remains a prominent and influential right-wing politician. Blocher called for Hildebrand to resign recently, arguing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had speculated with public funds when it attempted to limit the franc's rise by buying euros.\par \par \uc2 In calling for a peg, the left-wing is playing to Switzerland's exporters, who are watching their profit margins dwindle under the strong franc. Data from May showed a rise in Swiss exports, though prices for exported goods continued to drop due to the strong franc. Switzerland's exporters have been particularly vocal about demanding action from the SNB.\par \par \uc2 A franc peg such as the one China maintains to the U.S. dollar would mean Switzerland would also give up its independence over its monetary policy, something Swiss authorities are seen as loathe to do. The Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  currently allows the yuan to float up to 0.5% above or below a daily central parity rate against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Last Friday, SNB president Hildebrand forcefully asserted the SNB's right to act independently, saying its flexibility had been a valuable asset during the financial crisis, when it issued SNB bills to mop up excess money-market liquidity.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for the SNB didn't comment Monday.\par \par \uc2 Swiss business lobby Economiesuisse rejected the idea of a franc peg, saying the instrument would do little to ease exporters' current pain.\par \par \uc2 "The main problem is that we have an overvalued franc, but if we pegged a clearly overvalued franc at current levels, it would effectively perpetuate this overvaluation, which certainly isn't the best thing for Switzerland's exporters," Economiesuisse chief economist Rudolf Minsch said.\par \par \uc2 A study released Monday suggested Switzerland's exporters are adapting to the franc's strength more nimbly than their chorus of protests would lead one to believe. Specialization and continued high demand for Swiss products are reducing the impact of the rising Swiss franc on the country's exporters, according to the study, carried out by think-tank KOF.\par \par \uc2 -By Katharina Bart, Dow Jones Newswires; +41 43 443 8043; katharina.bart@dowjones.com [ 27-06-11 1444GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110627e76r00103\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
