\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: SNB Danthine: Sole  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Aim Was Price Stability\b0\par\par\uc2 598 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2011\par 19:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from Danthine in 9th, 10th and 11th paragraphs and comments from Dewet Moser in the last paragraph.)\par \par \uc2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's decision to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in 2009 and 2010 was aimed solely at maintaining price stability with deflation being the main risk at the time, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governing Board member \uc2 Jean-Pierre Danthine\uc2  said Thursday.\par \par \uc2 "At the start of 2009, the severe downturn in the global economy had translated into a very serious threat of deflation in Switzerland, and price stability was not assured," Danthine said in a speech prepared for delivery at a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  conference in Zurich.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the "substantial increase in the Swiss franc's value since the start of the financial crisis represented an inappropriate tightening of monetary conditions when it was imperative they be kept as loose as possible," Danthine said.\par \par \uc2 The SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly between March 2009 and June 2010, amassing large foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, most of them denominated in euros, which then depreciated. This resulted in a book loss of 19.2 billion Swiss francs ($21 billion) last year, which drew some public criticism in \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Danthine, whose speech focused on the SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried out during that period, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  judged the Swiss economy wouldn't have been able to withstand "a further violent appreciation of the franc [versus the euro]" at the time.\par \par \uc2 "By the end of May 2010, pressures on the franc started to subside, and the SNB considered in its June monetary assessment that the threat of deflation had largely disappeared," he said.\par \par \uc2 Danthine refuted suggestions the SNB "had been defeated by the [\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] markets" and said the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  provided the economy with monetary conditions that have contributed to a "reasonably swift recovery from the financial crisis, while assuring price stability."\par \par \uc2 The downturn in the Swiss economy has been less severe than in other countries and output has been the first to reach its pre-crisis level, he said. Swiss gross domestic product grew 2.6% in 2010, after declining 1.9% in 2009.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether Swiss interest rates will remain near zero for longer than expected because of developments elsewhere in Europe, Danthine said there are already signs of inflationary pressure building in the eurozone, as well as the U.K.\par \par \uc2 "European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet has warned about inflation picking up, and the U.K. may move on rates," he said.\par \par \uc2 As far as Switzerland is concerned, the SNB is evaluating the economic situation and the strength of the economy.\par \par \uc2 The SNB also reiterated that its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  weren't aimed at achieving any particular exchange-rate target, but rather to provide the most appropriate monetary conditions.\par \par \uc2 "The primary indicator of the success of the SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the inflation chart, and not the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance sheet," Danthine said. "Our \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  will only have been costly if they result in serious inflationary pressures two to three years from now."\par \par \uc2 Danthine said the SNB has, since June 2010, been absorbing the excess liquidity generated by the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the use of reverse repos and bills of various maturities.\par \par \uc2 In a separate speech, Dewet Moser, alternate member of the governing board of the SNB, said that the SNB bills have helped to bind liquidity. He said that these steps are forming the base for a gradual normalization in the money market.\par \par \uc2 -By Neil MacLucas, Dow Jones Newswires, +41 43 443 80 46; neil.maclucas@dowjones.com [ 24-03-11 1835GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110324e73o0017n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
