\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Little Room Seen For SNB  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,166 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2011\par 11:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1025 GMT [Dow Jones] Talk the SNB is \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the dollar is shrugged off as unlikely. "The past \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have been criticized a lot in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Switzerland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . And now politics is involved," a Zurich trader says. "This makes any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unlikely. Also, the fact that the franc has come off highs against the euro and the dollar has reduced the need for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," he says. SNB declined to comment. USD/CHF at 0.9428 and EUR/CHF at 1.2872. (goran.mijuk@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1022 GMT [Dow Jones]--KOF's employment indicator falls in January to 7.5 points, from a revised 10.2 in October, meaning the gradual rise has been stopped. "Despite the decline, the continued positive value of the indicator shows Swiss firms intend to keep hiring more staff," think-tank KOF says. Swiss December jobless rate was 3.8%. (katharina.bart@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1022 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts have drifted lower in light volumes. A London-based dealer says certain players had gone into the weekend with long positions on core government bonds which they are shedding now, with an added excuse coming from a hawkish article from MPC member Weale. "While the GDP numbers last week were poor, there is still a likelihood of an upward revision down the line and I would still keep a short-core view," the trader says. March gilts are down 0.15 at 117.19. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1021 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  recommends paying HUF 2-year rates. Says the curve is pricing in only 50 bps of rate increases by end-2012 from the current 6.00%, versus \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 's expectation of 125 bps. Adds even if the new members of the Monetary Policy Council turn out to be dovish, some risk premium needs to be priced in to the curve. Also recommends playing the broad EUR/HUF 270-290 range seen over the past eight months. EUR/HUF trades at 273.00. Adds government policy has tended to be detrimental for the HUF, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has acted to support it. Says the government's structural fiscal reform package in February could boost the HUF. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1013 GMT [Dow Jones] US Treasurys have kept most of Friday's risk-aversion gains as concerns intensify over the political tension in Egypt. 10-year US Treasurys currently yield 3.34%, after having traded around 3.46% at the end of last week, while the March Treasury contract is trading 0.015 lower at 121.035. There is a light data slate on Monday with Personal income, spending and PCE core prices at 1330 GMT and the market is already looking ahead to Friday's employment report. (nick.cawley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1010 GMT [Dow Jones] Norway retail sales, excluding motor vehicles, come in down 2%, which was "weak" and lower than expected, says Nordea. These number were "probably a reaction down after the strong growth in retail sales and consumption of goods in November and to the high electricity prices at the end of 2010," Nordea says. The latter will probably also dampen growth in retail sales in January, the bank adds. (charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1007 GMT [Dow Jones] Norway's retail sales, excluding motor vehicles, fall 2% on the month in December but remain strong reflecting "the exceptional strength of private consumption in 4Q," says Handelsbanken. The retreat in retail sales to the same level seen in October suggest November strength "may have been a case of unusually early Christmas shopping," says Handelsbanken. Cold weather made for a 5% jump in electricity and fuel consumption on top of the 7.5% rise from October to November, the bank notes, while the drop in automobile registrations was larger than expected. (charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1007 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD adds some 15 ticks to the day's high of 1.3662 after the euro-zone January flash inflation reading came in at 2.4% versus 2.2% in December. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1003 GMT [Dow Jones] The tensions in Egypt are resulting in uncertainty now, says \uc2 Danske Bank\uc2 , but a more positive scenario could still result. "In a negative scenario tensions spread across the Middle East, which leads to disruptions in oil production and a spike in oil prices. In a positive scenario there is a relatively peaceful transition to a democratic government in Egypt, which would be viewed as rather positive by the markets," the bank notes. Adds the development in oil prices will be key to the further development in global financial markets. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1002 GMT [Dow Jones] CZK trades lower at 24.250-24.272 against the euro from 24.22 late Friday but firmer from 24.300 earlier Monday. "The koruna and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region are slightly weaker, in part driven by global security concerns," a local trader says. "There are concerns that problems in Egypt may spread to other areas in the Middle East and investors are seen opting for safe assets such as the Swiss franc and the dollar." (leos.rousek@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1001 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares end down 1.8% at 964.10 in moderate trade. Weakness in overseas bourses drags sentiment while absence of fresh positive cue and the upcoming Lunar Lunar New Year holidays reduce the trading appetite, says Asia Plus Securities' Senior Vice President Therdsak Thaveeteeratham. He tips strong support at 950 as the P/E ratio is around 12X, which is the normal trading level of the Thai shares, and the medium term investors are likely to accumulate the dividend plays. Resistance is tipped at 980. Among most actively traded shares, PTT (PTT.TH) is down 2.1% at THB335, Banpu (BANPU.TH) is down 3.2% at THB730, \uc2 Krung Thai Bank\uc2  (KTB.TH) is down 6% at THB15.80, \uc2 True Corporation\uc2  (TRUE.TH) is down 5.8% at THB6.50, \uc2 Indorama Ventures\uc2  (IVL.TH) is flat at THB39. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0958 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/THB is lower at 31.03 vs 31.08 late Friday. The pair hit a session high of to 30.18, as the USD strengthened on the back of the turmoil in Egypt. "However, the dollar/baht soon fell as buying interests fade, while those who bought (the dollar) earlier in the day switched sides. It's a fairly volatile session today," says Bangkok-based dealer. Still, the downside is likely to be limited as interbank players, importers buy just above 30.00. The pair is expected to trade in a 30.95-31.15 range tomorrow. Players will keep a close eye on developments in Egypt and political situation at home for trading cues. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com) [ 01-31-11 0525ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110131e71v00068\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
