\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ UPDATE: Swiss  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Reserves Drop As SNB Reins In  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 357 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2010\par 11:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves declined slightly in September, slipping for a fourth straight month, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 The value of the reserves fell by 2.7 billion Swiss francs ($2.8 billion) to CHF215.3 billion at the end of September, according to SNB data released Wednesday. This signals the buying of euros to check its depreciation versus the franc continued to tail off from the levels seen earlier this year, and as the dollar lost around 4.5% of its franc value in September.\par \par \uc2 "This drop in the reserve valuation is no surprise and extends the trend seen in recent months," according to Ulrich Leuchtmann, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 Commerzbank AG\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly earlier this year to try and counter the franc's excessive gains against the euro, citing the danger of deflation, though it appeared to alter this stance in June, saying such risks had disappeared.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  posted a hefty CHF2.8 billion book loss in the first half of the year as the franc's 10% appreciation against the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  eroded the value of its euro holdings.\par \par \uc2 While the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has indicated the risk of deflation is diminishing, consumer price data Tuesday showed Swiss monthly inflation held at zero for a second month in September.\par \par \uc2 "The CPI report was weaker than expected, and while deflation isn't a threat right now, such risks may emerge next year," said Leuchtmann.\par \par \uc2 The SNB in September lowered its forecast for average Swiss inflation to 0.7% for 2010, and to 0.3% in 2011, and said the possibility of negative inflation at the start of next year can't be excluded.\par \par \uc2 At 0955 GMT, the Swiss franc was quoted at 1.3387 against the euro, and at 0.9655 versus the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Website: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.snb.ch"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.snb.ch}}}\par \par \uc2 -By Neil MacLucas, Dow Jones Newswires, +41 43 443 8046;\par \par \uc2 neil.mclucas@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 06, 2010 05:02 ET (09:02 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020101006e6a6001md\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
