\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: EUR/CHF Near Low, Shows No Signs Of Reversal\b0\par\par\uc2 1,741 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2010\par 08:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0631 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF is trading just above its record low of 1.2850 but is showing no signs of any reversal of its downside trend, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Technical analyst Karen Anne Jones says the next "psychological" support level is at 1.2750. The outlook for USD/CHF is also negative as long as it stays below 1.0326, she adds. EUR/CHF at 1.2922 vs a low of 1.2863. USD/CHF at 1.0159 from a high of 1.01815. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0628 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mostly higher; Nikkei +1.2%, S&P/ASX 200 +2.1%, Kospi +1.3%, HSI +0.4%, Sensex +0.7%, Shanghai Composite down 1.0%, STI +0.7%, NZX-50 +1.4%, Taiex +0.7%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, JPY loses steam vs USD, EUR on higher stock markets; statement from DPJ leadership candidate favoring \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb JPY strength also weighs. EUR/USD at 1.2706 vs 1.2673 late NY, EUR/JPY at 107.25 vs 106.25, USD/JPY at 84.37 vs 83.83. Democratic Party of Japan's Ozawa, one of party's most powerful members, submits candidacy for party leadership, setting up battle with incumbent PM Kan; vote set for Sept. 14., winner will be nation's PM. Ozawa says "we will boldly take all measures, including market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " to peg back yen, in policy statement reported by Kyodo News. Australia 2Q GDP +1.2% on-quarter, +3.3% on year, expected +0.9% on-quarter, +2.8% on-year. August PMI fell 2.7 points from July to 51.7. GDP data boost AUD/USD, last 0.8999 vs 0.8948 pre-data. China's official August PMI at 51.7 vs 51.2 in July; \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's measure for August at 51.9 vs 49.4 in July. South Korea August trade surplus down at $2.08 billion vs $5.51 billion July, $2.17 billion forecast; exports rose weaker-than-expected 29.6% on year, imports +29.3%. Korea August CPI +0.3% on-month, same pace as July, vs +0.4% forecast; August \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI at 50.9 vs 53.2 July. Taiwan August \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI fell to 49.2 vs 50.5 July; contracting for first time in 18 months. India \uc2 HSBC\uc2  August PMI at 57.2 vs 57.6 in July. Spot gold at $1,248.80/oz, up $1.10 from late New York trade Monday. October Nymex crude oil futures up 49 cents at $72.41/bbl. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0626 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF is likely to remain under pressure as long as risk aversion prevails and the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  doesn't signal any intention to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . "The Swiss economic recovery is robust, which \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  GDP data Thursday is likely to confirm and we expect this also to be reflected in consumer prices," data due Friday, says analyst Lutz Karpowitz. "If CPI disappoints, speculation the SNB will alter its stance could increase," he adds. EUR/CHF at 1.2919 vs a low of 1.2863. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0621 GMT [Dow Jones] September bunds are down 0.25 at 134.18, after hitting another record high at 134.77 in the previous session, which is impressive given the already strong recent rises, says \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2 . However, bunds are likely to consolidate Wednesday and \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2  pegs immediate support at 134.00, followed by 133.25. Resistance is at 134.77 and 135.00. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0621 GMT [Dow Jones] China's official PMI for August -- up at 51.7 vs July's 51.2 -- keeping Asian stocks higher, as it offsets disappointing PMIs elsewhere in the region, with Taiwan \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI in contraction territory (below 50) for first time in 18 months, Korea's \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI slows for fourth consecutive month and at 17-month low, at 50.9 in August, down from 53.2 in July. India's \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI, just released, also came in below July's, at 57.25 vs 57.6. Concerns on export-related woes in Taiwan and first signs of trouble after sterling multi-quarter growth in Korea closely tied to views that U.S. economy may flirt with double dip, but China carries on regardless; "PMI shows that manufacturing sector activity accelerated modestly in August and that the expansion in manufacturing activity is likely to continue in the months ahead," Nomura says. China stocks, Asia's worst performing this year, also go on as before this year, with Shanghai stocks only ones in negative territory in region today, last down 0.8%. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0617 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares reverse gains as investors taking profit in small- and medium-caps outweigh PMI-induced gains in heavyweights on renewed confidence in pace of economic growth. Shanghai Composite Index down 0.8% at 2616.64, tipped to test immediate support at 2600 rest of session. "The heavyweights are holding steady, with banks making gains after PMI came out, but because the small- and medium-cap shares have risen way above their valuations, profit-taking pressure was very high," says Qian Qimin at Shenyin Wanguo Securities. The Shanghai small- and medium-caps index now down 2.2% at 6047.23 after rising 24% since early July. Shenzhen Composite Index down 1.9% at 1144.0. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0614 GMT [Dow Jones] China rice harvest emerges as greatest uncertainty in government quest for seventh record grain output this year, causing considerable official unease over food security. Falling early-season rice output threatens overall crop size, prompts state initiatives, higher subsidies, increased paddy acreage. China still has strong policy defenses to buffer market volatility, including largest grain reserves in the world, but rising demand, uncertain supply erode insulation. State \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  shows results but protection also produces domestic price premiums that encourage higher imports, underscores Beijing's policy dilemma: The need to maintain long-term self-sufficiency in the face of cheaper imports. See: =FOCUS: "Smaller Rice Crop To Slow China Grain Output Growth." (chuin-wei.yap@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0609 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume +36.2% at HK$1.826 billion midday, 5.8% of total market volume vs 5.3% at Tuesday's half-day, although full-day ratio unlikely to be higher than Tuesday's 8.2%, as HSI's 0.4% rebound expected to keep some bears on sidelines. Li Ning (2331.HK) leads with HK$149.27 million, followed by China Mobile (0941.HK) with HK$126.50 million. Rest of top 5 don't manage HK$100 million: \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (3968.HK) has HK$87.29 million, Hutchison (0013.HK) HK$72.19 million, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) HK$71.09 million. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0600 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end +0.7% at 7668.25, tracking Wall Street's moderate gains overnight. Taiex likely to stay in tight 7650-7700 range tomorrow, as market lacks fresh trading leads as earnings season over, says IBT Asset Management manager Ernest Chiang. "Market sentiment remains bearish as tech companies provided weak outlooks for the third quarter. Investors also continue to worry about prospects for the U.S. economy." PC makers up on strong 2Q results: Compal (2324.TW) +1.4% at NT$36.40, Acer (2353.TW) +2% at NT$76.60. Heavyweight Hon Hai (2317.TW) down 4% at NT$108.50 on murky 2H outlook, extending yesterday's 6.6% fall. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0556 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures sustain losses in afternoon trade as foreigners remain net sellers in futures, while investors grow increasingly cautious over possible key policy rate hike at next week's BOK monetary policy meeting, participants say; 3-year yield quoted +7 bps at 3.62%, 5-year yield +4 bps at 4.04%, 10-year yield +4 bps at 4.42%, September futures down 15 ticks at 112.04. Rate hike expectations continue to weigh on shorter-end bonds, especially 3-year treasuries for today, says bond manager at local securities firm. Notes, yield spread between 3-year and 5-year bonds and spread between 3-year and 10-year bonds had narrowed abruptly in recent sessions, leading to flatter yield curve, and it seems about time for curve to see some steepening, but rate hike chances keeping yield curve from steepening. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0554 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan bonds of longer durations continue to rally, with 10-year yield hitting new all-time low, as investors flee for safety amid continued worries about sustainability of global economic recovery, local trader says; but short-ends unmoved on expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may hike policy rate - probably by another 12.5 bps - later this month. 10-year yield ends at 1.1382% vs all-time low of 1.1328% hit earlier, 1.1454% last close; tips firm floor at 1.1000%. "In the near term, yields (of longer duration) will likely continue to fall." 5-year yield flat at 0.8500% vs yesterday's close, after trading in 0.8370%-0.8650% range. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0546 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.4% at 917.13 midday in heavy volume after hitting 924.88, highest intraday level hit since Nov 1, 2007. Big cap energy, petrochemical plays mostly higher ahead of court's ruling (due Thursday) on fate of suspended projects in Map Ta Phut, says analyst with BFIT Securities Aphisit Limsupanark. "Retail investors are switching from small- and mid-cap stocks to big-cap stocks" as foreign fund inflows continue, he adds. Resistance tipped at 924-925, support at 913-914. Among top actives, PTT (PTT.TH) +1.5% at THB269, TMB Bank (TMB.TH) off 1.5% at THB2.66, Banpu (BANPU.TH) +1.3% at THB620, \uc2 Krung Thai Bank\uc2  (KTB.TH) +0.7% at THB14.50, \uc2 Siam Cement\uc2  (SCC.TH) +1.0% at THB299. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com) [ 09-01-10 0231ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100901e69100053\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
