\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Focus: The Waiting Game On  \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland \highlight\uc2  And Japan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Martin   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 873 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2010\par 09:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Which \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets first: the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  or the Bank of Japan?\par \par \uc2 The chance that either will come into the market to tackle the soaring yen or franc is enough to put many traders off buying either \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar or the euro.\par \par \uc2 That means that buying one against the other in a "who will blink first?" trade is happy hunting ground for the brave.\par \par \uc2 This is, perhaps, a sign of how desperate \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts are getting for good trades. Almost all major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  [and indeed commodity-linked and emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  too] are flashing bright red sell signals at the moment, so analysts and investors are forced to come up with some pretty creative ideas.\par \par \uc2 It does make sense, though, so it's worth weighing up the two.\par \par \uc2 By one measure, the Swiss are the more likely to take the plunge. After all, in real terms, the franc is stronger now than the yen, \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  pointed out in a note to clients last week. Some market-watchers believe that means the Japanese authorities will sit back and watch the yen climb much more--as long as it's a slow and steady move--before they try to flog it back down.\par \par \uc2 Still, even though the franc is now at an all-time high against the euro, which Monday sank below CHF1.2950 for the first time, few if any serious franc-watchers think a sting is coming.\par \par \uc2 That's partly because the Swiss economy keeps on proving itself perfectly capable of taking this strain, despite the risk to exports. Friday, the country's KOF index--a key leading indicator--did edge lower, suggesting that the country's economic recovery will slow for the rest of the year. Still, it was not a sharp pullback, and it comes after the index hit its highest level in four years in July.\par \par \uc2 If franc strength is seriously damaging, then that's not clear in the KOF think-tank's report, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still reasonably likely to raise interest rates at some point late in 2010.\par \par \uc2 What's more, the SNB has already tried a heavy-duty program of franc selling this year, and it didn't exactly work, although one can argue that the franc would be much higher now if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hadn't worked so hard to hold it down. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly during April and May when it was swimming against a heavy tide of euro weakness, "were a mistake," \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 So, rule \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of the race to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and buy the franc against the yen, the French bank concludes.\par \par \uc2 The chorus of political pressure in Japan for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  there to do something, anything, about yen strength is certainly pretty loud, and indeed, further easing measures are starting to emerge.\par \par \uc2 At an emergency meeting Monday, the Bank Of Japan's policy board voted to offer domestic financial institutions Y10 trillion of six-month loans at an ultralow rate of 0.1%. This was in addition to the Y20 trillion in three-month loans it had already been offering. We are constantly reminded that the authorities in Tokyo are watching the yen's climb with intense interest. Right now, though, the market is skeptical, and traders are continuing to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher.\par \par \uc2 Direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , of the kind that is being threatened probably wouldn't work for long, particularly if Japan dived into the market without the help of the U.S. or the euro zone. It would also undermine international efforts to convince China to let the yuan climb. Still, a Japanese zap on the yen does seem somewhat more likely than a Swiss move. If the worst comes to the worst, and neither blinked, selling the yen against the franc in anticipation of such a development is a cheap bet to run.\par \par \uc2 In early European trading Tuesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was showing clear signs of nerves, with the yen and the franc--both seen as safe havens in times of stress--moving higher.\par \par \uc2 The euro was at CHF1.2940, according to trading system EBS, having dipped to a fresh record low of CHF1.2932 in Asian trading hours. It was at around CHF1.30 late in New York Monday.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was at Y84.12 against the yen, from around Y84.75.\par \par \uc2 The euro was weaker at $1.2643 from $1.2675, while sterling was also lower at $1.5431, from $1.5466.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg TNI FRX POV\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Reuters   USD/DJ \par    Thomson   P/1066 or P/1074 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Katie Martin, Dow Jones Newswires, +44-20-7842-9346; katie.martin@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers 