\par\par \b \uc2 SNB WATCH: Focus On Swiss Franc  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 , Less On Rates\b0\par\par\uc2 By Martin Gelnar  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 729 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 2010\par 16:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will in all probability leave interest rates unchanged at its upcoming quarterly meeting but the focus will be on its stance on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There is little doubt the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  will stick with its target of 0.25% for the three-month Swiss franc London Interbank Offered Rate at its quarterly policy review June 17, in line with recent no-change decisions by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of England.\par \par \uc2 The SNB's expected decision to stand pat will come despite signs of a clear economic rebound, setting Switzerland apart from many European neighbors where economic activity is slow to revive. However, the effect on Switzerland of worsening concerns about government debts in part of the euro zone would make any hike in Swiss rates premature, most economists say. Plus, any rise in Swiss rates--a step most market watchers expect towards the year at the earliest--would put additional upward pressure on the franc.\par \par \uc2 Therefore, markets now focus on whether the SNB will alter its position on \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, a tool it has used on a massive scale for more than a year to keep the franc under control.\par \par \uc2 The dilemma for the SNB twofold: It can't very well hike interest rates lest such a move propel the Swiss franc to further highs, nor is it likely to easily abandon its heavy-handed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  management for the same reason.\par \par \uc2 While many onlookers expects the SNB to renew its pledge to fight any excessive Swiss franc strength, there isn't a consensus. "The SNB is likely to announce the end of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , since it has been unable to keep the franc from appreciating strongly as investors seek haven assets," \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Economy.com economist Melanie Bowler said.\par \par \uc2 Since March 2009, the SNB has tried to prevent a massive strengthening of the franc against the euro, spending billions of francs to buy the common Europeancurrencyy. However, the policy has been only partly successful.\par \par \uc2 The SNB's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have swollen to CHF232 billion by the end of May from slightly below CHF100 billion at the end of 2009, leaving some market watchers wondering how much longer the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is willing to carry on with such unusual measures.\par \par \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Securities recently pointed out that Swiss reserves are now on a par with those of China relative to the size of the two countries' economies.\par \par \uc2 The current level of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves is largely uncharted territory for the SNB and the current surge of euro holdings makes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  vulnerable to swings in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, some observers have said.\par \par \uc2 Peter Bernholz, a former economics professor at the University of Basel, recently suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should build up its gold reserves rather than focusing on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gold, currently a vastly popular investment, has recently drawn interest from at least one \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 : In late 2009, the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  bought 200 tons of gold from the International Monetary Fund, citing efforts to diversify its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the SNB has in the past few years reduced its gold holdings under a coordinated gold-selling program by several central banks. It currently owns 1,040 tons of gold.\par \par \uc2 Despite the SNB's robust efforts to manage the franc, the euro currently stands at around CHF1.39, close to a recent record low of CHF1.3746. The euro traded at CHF1.50 just a few months ago. But without the SNB's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro could stand as low as CHF1.20, some economists have estimated.\par \par \uc2 The current level of exchange rates is a serious problem for the country's important export industry because the strong franc makes Swiss goods more expensive.\par \par \uc2 At the quarterly meeting on Thursday, the SNB will also provide an update of its forecasts for economic growth and inflation. Currently, it expects the country's GDP to increase at a rate of about 1.5% this year and forecasts average yearly inflation to stand at 0.7% in 2010, 0.9% next year and 2.2% in 2012.\par \par \uc2 -By Martin Gelnar, Dow Jones Newswires; +41 43 443 8042; martin.gelnar@dowjones.com [ 14-06-10 1426GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100614e66e000wy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
