\par\par \b \uc2 COLUMN-Swiss lessons in spitting in the wind: James Saft\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jim Saft   \par \uc2 899 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2010\par 21:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (James Saft is a Reuters columnist. The opinions expressed are his own)\par \par \uc2 HUNTSVILLE, Ala. (Reuters) - Looking at Switzerland's massive efforts to stem the effects of the euro meltdown on its economy, two things are clear: it is a ruinously expensive undertaking and the most direct beneficiaries are speculators.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland devoted the equivalent of 15 percent of annual gross domestic product in May alone building up reserves as it sought to reverse the rise of its franc against the euro. Only China, which has an economy 33 times the size of Switzerland, has ever racked up more than the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's 67.7 billion francs in reserves in a month. The Swiss then have deployed something approaching 45 percent of annual GDP since the end of the year seeking to cap strength of the franc.\par \par \uc2 And the result? Well, the euro has only lost about 7 percent against the franc this year as against a far deeper loss against the dollar, but the trend has been inexorable. Time and again traders have challenged the Swiss authorities and met, after a time, with success.\par \par \uc2 This of course raises the question of what might happen should the Swiss think better of spending thousands per capita on lining the pockets of speculators, or investors if you please. The result, while perhaps inevitable, would not be pretty.\par \par \uc2 As ever with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the problem is that authorities set the markets a target and supply it with a deep-pocketed trading partner. Unless \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is coordinated across nations and truly massive, the result is often to attract more capital to take the other side of the trade. To be truly effective, a unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably has to be unsterilized, meaning that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doing the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  allows its money supply to balloon and risks losing control off its own monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 To understand the spitting-in-the-wind nature of Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  you only have to realize that \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in marked contrast to its euro zone neighbors, has both fiscal and trade surpluses. It also has a debt to GDP ratio that is a modest 45 percent. In other words, Switzerland has been doing more or less the right things.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Mansoor Mohi-uddin of \uc2 UBS\uc2  cleverly compares the Swiss franc to the German deutschmark of yore, and it certainly must on its fundamentals evoke a kind of nostalgia among Germans who remember the good old days of sound money.\par \par \uc2 So as ever, the speculators aren't really speculators but simply investors who recognize that there are strong fundamentals and an official buyer with plenty of money. In that way speculators are a bit like weeds, the definition of which is a plant that observer wishes was not growing there.\par \par \uc2 HUNGARIAN MORTGAGES\par \par \uc2 So, why are the Swiss spending all of this money trying to fix a problem that is not of their making and is probably far too big for their economy to effectively counter?\par \par \uc2 Besides making Swiss exports less competitive and threatening its recovery, an important threat of a strong franc is that by lowering the price of imported goods it may allow a self-reinforcing deflation to take hold. With Swiss official interest rates at 0.25 percent and consumer price inflation at 1.1 percent and falling that is no idle threat.\par \par \uc2 With interest rates so low and a small domestic bond market, the reserve accumulation is really a form of quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 A strengthening Swiss franc, especially against central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Hungarian forint, also causes problems for Swiss and other banks. In a desperate attempt to get on the property ladder many Hungarian borrowers took out mortgages denominated in Swiss francs, which then had much lower interest rates. This account for about 60 percent of the stock of outstanding Hungarian mortgages. That allowed them to buy far more house than they could really afford and also exposed them to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. As the franc rises so does what they owe, though now that things are not looking as rosy in central Europe they may choose to default.\par \par \uc2 And while most of those franc loans were actually made by non-Swiss banks, the potential for contagion and damage is strong.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland's problems with a weak euro and potentially disintegrating euro zone are simply the problems of the rest of the world writ large. A falling euro will spread deflation, hit exports, depress consumption and further stress the banking system.\par \par \uc2 It would be dangerous to suppose that because Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not successful that the United States will be similarly toothless if they choose to wade in. If, and it is a massive if, the United States were able to convince European authorities that joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were needed it would probably be successful. Would the European authorities cooperate? Perhaps.\par \par \uc2 The United States might choose to go it alone, but that risks the very destabilizing effects of failure. (Editing by James Dalgleish) (At the time of publication James Saft did not own any direct investments in securities mentioned in this article. He may be an owner indirectly as an investor in a fund. You can email him at jamessaft@jamessaft.com and find more columns at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.reuters.com/news/globalcoverage/columns"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.reuters.com/news/globalcoverage/columns}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 COLUMN-MARKETS/SAFT|LANGEN|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100610e66a001l3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
