\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro edges up vs dollar; pound dips on debt warning\b0\par\par\uc2 760 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 2010\par 02:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Investors stay jittery over euro zone debt crisis\par \par \uc2 * Euro hits lifetime low vs Swiss franc\par \par \uc2 * Fed policymaker says Europe's woes no big threat to U.S.\par \par \uc2 * Sterling slips on Fitch's UK debt warning (Adds details, changes byline)\par \par \uc2 By Steven C. Johnson and Vivianne Rodrigues\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, June 8 (Reuters) - The euro rose on Tuesday as investors booked profits a day after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit its lowest level against the dollar since early 2006, and the pound fell after a ratings agency urged Britain to cut its deficit.\par \par \uc2 The euro also hit an all-time trough below 1.38 Swiss francs but rebounded sharply, with traders citing \uc2 Swiss National Bank\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the franc. The SNB declined to comment. For details, see [ID:nWEA5363].\par \par \uc2 Against the dollar, the euro rose above $1.20 after tumbling to $1.1876 on Monday, its lowest level since March 2006. But analysts said the market was still anxious about debt levels in several euro zone countries and debt auctions this week from Portugal and Spain.\par \par \uc2 "The euro decline isn't over," said Marc Chandler, senior strategist at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  in New York. "There are supply concerns this week, and what we're seeing now is a brief respite. A rise above $1.20 would be a good chance to sell."\par \par \uc2 In late afternoon trading in New York, the euro was up 0.4 percent at $1.1954 <EUR=> after hitting a session peak at $1.2008, according to Reuters data. Analysts said it also saw support after euro zone ministers made final arrangements Monday to set up funds for countries facing debt problems. It was up 0.3 percent at 109.15 yen <EURJPY=>.\par \par \uc2 The euro has shed more than 16 percent against the dollar this year, and some economists worry this will hurt U.S. exports to the euro zone, a fear Chicago Federal Reserve President Charles Evans downplayed on Tuesday. [ID:nN08177384]\par \par \uc2 SWISS SPECULATION, UK DEBT WORRIES\par \par \uc2 The euro's abrupt rebound from a low against the Swiss franc earlier spurred talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 2009 to prevent excess franc strength but slowed its euro purchases recently as the euro fell below 1.40 francs.\par \par \uc2 The euro was last 0.6 percent lower at 1.3759 <EURCHF=>.\par \par \uc2 "Certainly the price action minutes ago seemed to suggest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . though we've no official confirmation," said one market participant at a U.S.-based bank about the euro's jump.\par \par \uc2 Sterling fell 0.3 percent to $1.4418 <GBP=D4> after Fitch Ratings said the UK was facing a "formidable" fiscal challenge and said Britain's public debt ratio had climbed more quickly than those of other top-rated sovereign credits. [ID:nWLA5820]\par \par \uc2 "It's more of the contagion fear that's been gripping markets for months now," said John Doyle, strategist at Tempus Consulting in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar was flat at 91.34 <JPY=>. Earlier, new Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan chose a fiscal conservative as his finance minister. Kan has in the past advocated a weaker yen to help Japanese exports and fight deflation, but Chandler said that might be wishful thinking as long as markets are in crisis mode.\par \par \uc2 During times of risk aversion, the yen tends to rise as investors exit positions in riskier \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and assets.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, technical analyst Robert Prechter told the Reuters Investment Outlook Summit in New York that the euro is likely to bottom out against the dollar within two weeks.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, though, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  strategists said they expect risk aversion to continue to hold sway in markets. [ID:nN07198050]\par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has yet to bottom out against the dollar and may eventually reach parity, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  chief global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Steven Englander said at the Reuters Investment Outlook Summit in New York on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Englander sees the euro between $1.10 and $1.15 over the next one to three months. However, a weaker euro would help to offset slower growth in euro zone countries such as Greece and Spain, which will eventually help the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recover.\par \par \uc2 In the long run, "I think time is on the side of the euro," he said, provided countries stick to austerity budgets.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Naomi Tajitsu in London; Editing by Chizu Nomiyama)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 9)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|J|UKI|Z|CANT|AUF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100608e6680000c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
