\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: GBP/CHF Selling Weighs On EUR/CHF - Trader\b0\par\par\uc2 1,133 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Mai 2010\par 10:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0809 GMT [Dow Jones] A large GBP/CHF sell order did EUR/CHF no favors this morning, says a trader. GBP/CHF now trades at 1.6470 from the day's high of 1.6550, while EUR/CHF has fallen to 1.4006 from its 1.4070 high. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0804 GMT [Dow Jones] Downside risks for the EUR and the GBP are more on the crosses than against the USD, warns Standard Bank. It notes that while the ECB and the BoE are now both showing signs of "playing a bit fast and loose with inflation" with both Bund and gilt yields likely to rise, the Fed itself is hardly a bastion of monetary discipline right now. "The biggest dangers for the GBP and the EUR lie on the crosses," the bank says. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0804 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF comes within 4 ticks of a fresh all- time low and then bounces from 1.4008 to 1.4025. A UK bank trader says there is a good-sized bid in the market but he does not think it's from the SNB. It's a public holiday in \b \highlight22\uc2 Switzerland\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday although that would not stop the SNB from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or leaving an order with another \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or large institution. EUR/CHF now slipping again at 1.4011. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0800 GMT [Dow Jones] Negative correlation between gold, USD likely to return as fears ease over euro zone stabilisation package, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Gold normally falls when USD rises, but pattern broken by recent safe-haven flows into metal sparked by euro zone troubles. However, BarCap sees gold correlation with trade-weighted DXY dollar index falling "back into negative territory". Adds, "periods of positive gold-DXY correlation also coincide with large swings in the global equity markets". EUR/USD last at $1.2635, steady vs 1.2629 late NY trade Wednesday; gold at $1,235.30/oz, up 2.2% from NY close. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0759 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS up on strength in domestic stocks (Shanghai Composite Index +2.1% at 2710.51); 1-year +1 bp at 2.08%-2.18%, 5-year +7 bps at 3.12%-3.22%, 1-to-5-year spread 6 bps wider at 104 bps. "But the swaps have very limited upside as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  intends to keep enough liquidity in the banking system," says Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. PBOC injects CNY152 billion into money market via open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week to avoid liquidity crunch after reserve ratio hike took effect May 10. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0758 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  expects "healthy" demand at Italy's up to EUR5Bln auction of five and 15-year BTP government bonds Thursday, given the better positioning of Italy among euro zone "periphery" and the subdued amount on auction. Strategists Luca Cazzulani and Chiara Cremonesi say the BTP 3% April 2015 is more interesting for curve trades rather than relative value as it trades pretty much in line with its neighbors. The 58bps in 10/15Y BTPs looks very steep, they say, suggesting taking advantage of this by selling BTP 4% September 2020 and buying BTP March 2025 at the auction to gain a yield pick-up. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0754 GMT [Dow Jones] GBP/USD breaches key 1.4860 support and slips quickly to 1.4840. Both BNP and Barclay Capital's chartists say a sustained break of this level calls the end of GBP/USD's recovery from its post-election lows of 1.4450, and opens risk back towards this area. The spot now trades at 1.4847. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0753 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF once again tested and held the 1.4025-00 area and \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Karen Jones says allow for a rebound Thursday. Now at 1.4040 she favors tiny longs at market, adding between 1.4025-00 with a tight stop below as failure of 1.4000 will open downside risk toward 1.3850 and the 1.3520. EUR/CHF now at 1.4020. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0751 GMT [Dow Jones] After weeks of being focused on the euro zone and the UK, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  spotlight may swivel back to the US later Thursday. An absence of any new developments in the debt crisis and a lack of any major economic data out of G10, means that speeches by the Fed's Kohn, Bernanke, Kocherlakota and Fisher will likely get even more attention than usual. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0749 GMT [Dow Jones] May HSI futures +0.4% at 20,230, lagging cash market's 0.9% gain, thus turning contract's 68-point discount to cash at Wednesday's close to around 160-point discount now, again wider than May fair value about 150-point discount, signalling slightly negative bias in market's near-term direction. Volume modest at 69,347 lots. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0743 GMT [Dow Jones] GBP/USD reversed sharply to the downside Wednesday after the BOE's dovish Quarterly Inflation Report notes \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . The bank says the spot has key support at 1.4860 and failure here will confirm that the recent rebound is complete, 1.5055 was a corrective top and a decline back to last week's 1.4475 lows is underway. Further out, the bank remains bearish on GBP/USD looking for a move towards 1.28 in the 1Q of 2011. GBP/USD now at 1.4870. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0743 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex remains firm, now +0.7% at 17,319.70, on buoyant Asian markets, positive U.S. markets with gains led by autos, banks. Mehul Dedhia, associate vice president at Mumbai-based brokerage Sharekhan, says "in the absence of negative news, our markets are trading higher." Market watchers say concerns over Europe debt crisis remain, investors will be keenly watching for any developments on that front. Dedhia says next major domestic trigger for markets would be monsoons. "India being an agrarian economy, monsoon does play a very big role." \uc2 Mahindra & Mahindra\uc2  (500520.BY) +4.3% at INR569.70, ICICI Bank (532174.BY) +1.3% at INR927, \uc2 Infosys (500209.BY) \uc2 +0.8% at INR2,703.55, \uc2 Jaiprakash Associates\uc2  (532532.BY) +3.1% at INR134.80. S&P CNX Nifty +0.7% at 5192.10. Dedhia expects volatile trading in near term, tips 5000-5300 band for Nifty. (amitha.rajan@dowjones.com) [ 05-13-10 0409ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100513e65d0005f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
