r Nifty. (amitha.rajan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0737 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts are down on the day as equities rise but less so than bunds, a sign that the optimism following the formation of the new government has not dissipated yet. However, a rebound in risky assets means gilts remain susceptible to unwinding of flight to safety bids. The political uncertainty that had followed last week's election ended after the Conservatives and the Lib Dems agreed a formal coalition Tuesday. The Bank of England has also signalled that its stance will stay accommodative for some time. The DMO auctions GBP1.1Bln UKT1.875% 2022 linker while trade data are out 0830 GMT. The 2s/10s gilt yield curve steepens to 296.4bps vs Wednesday's 294.1bps. June gilts are down 0.08 at 117.09 and the benchmark 10-year gilt is down at 107.31 to yield 3.847%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0731 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  recommends buying long PLN/HUF, favoring relative value trade. Says a wide basis in Central Europe suggests the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovery might not be viable especially in Hungary, where one-year swap to Euribor has gone further into negative territory. Says Hungary is heading toward a period of budget uncertainty on the new government's attempts to cut taxes whilst harshly criticising the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which should unsettle HUF assets. At the same time, Polish officials are defending a EUR/PLN 3.80-4.25 range, it adds. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com) [ 05-13-10 0404ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100513e65d0005b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
